	affiliations = {Department of Fashion, South Africa; University of Johannesburg, South Africa; Federal University of Lafia, Nigeria; Federal University of Lafia, Nigeria},
	abstract = {The Fourth Industrial Revolution(4IR), with its emphasis on the quest for more sophisticated technologies, not only hold beautiful promises for our world but also comes with some challenges. While some countries in the West are already committed to the domestication of artificial intelligence(AI), Africa still relies on other technologically advanced nations to meet its digital needs. Every global invention is, first of all, local. Consequently, the domicile of every invention has a major influence on the rules of practice. This claim calls to question the place of the African continent in the global applications of various inventions, where the continent is either sidelined or insignificant. How then could a global technology work in, e.g., the sub-Saharan African region when the rules of practice are generated from a different province? This paper interrogates the justifiability of what will be referred to as performative AI in Africa. We recommend intercultural model(s) that is/are remarkable in their application in Africa but, at the same time, universalisable. By this, we also acknowledge the possibilities that there could be similar models in other regions. Since dialogue is what we seek, we will use a method of conversation from Africa to interrogate our arguments. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Fred Dervin and Hamza R’boul; individual chapters, the contributors.},
