	affiliations = {School of Computing and Information Systems, University of Melbourne, Australia; University of Melbourne, Australia},
	abstract = {While the field of technical artificial intelligence (AI) safety focuses on building programming rules to prevent AI from causing harm, little attention has been paid to applying human-centered corrective mechanisms such as whistleblowing systems to AI when it does cause harms. This chapter proposes a cross-disciplinary approach, drawing on established mechanisms developed in the anti-corruption field and applying them to AI control. It maps identified AI risks against a framework of corruption activities. Viewing the AI control problems through this lens makes it possible to compare human-based solutions against programming solutions in future research more effectively. The chapter explores risks to humanity from ‘AI corruption,’ including hallucination, deceptiveness, jailbreaks, misuse, discrimination, and existential threats. Whistleblowing systems have proven effective in combating corruption across various settings. It is proposed that these can be adapted to address AI-related issues. In particular, whistleblowing has a unique role in addressing the ‘black-box’ nature of AI systems, where insiders with specialist knowledge can provide valuable insights that few others could see. The chapter concludes by proposing future research directions to better understand the dynamics of whistleblowing in the AI industry. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Sope Williams, Maria Krambia Kapardis, and Lisa A. Kihl; individual chapters, the contributors. All rights reserved.},
	keywords = {Artificial intelligence; Computer systems programming; Risk perception; Anti-corruption; Artificial intelligence systems; Black boxes; Control problems; Cross-disciplinary approaches; Future research directions; Integrity systems; Intelligence control; Programming solutions; Crime},
