	affiliations = {Xi’an Jiaotong-Liverpool University, China},
	abstract = {AI-driven surveillance platforms in Xinjiang are not only monitoring communities but also reshaping interethnic relations. While technology in governance has been widely studied, its role in transforming interethnic dynamics remains underexplored. Drawing on document analysis and digital ethnography, this paper situates surveillance within theories of algorithmic governance, arguing that smart governance platforms and predictive systems act as ‘sanitizing’ mediators. By filtering out overt conflict, they create an illusion of harmony while masking structural violence through racial profiling and re-education. Increasingly embedded in the Grid-Management System, big data and AI’s perceived neutrality legitimizes authoritarian control and renders dissent illegible. This study highlights how algorithmic systems produce a façade of social stability, reconfiguring Han-Uyghur relations in Xinjiang under the guise of promoting order. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
