	affiliations = {Lovely Professional University, School of Cse, Punjab, Phagwara, India},
	abstract = {Oral cancer is a major health issue all over the world, and the early diagnosis of the same will be important in enhancing the chances of survival of the patient. Deep learning based Artificial Intelligence (AI) models have demonstrated high potential in the ability to accurately identify oral cancer using medical images. However, the major problem of these technologies is that they have the ability to learn and even exaggerate the existing prejudices based on the data they are trained on. This will result in less accurate AI tools among some groups of individuals, which will exacerbate healthcare inequities rather than resolve them. The present review of the literature presents the current application of AI to detect oral cancer, particularly focusing on the issue of bias and fairness. We consider the origin of bias, e.g., unbalanced datasets, which are not diverse and ways of quantifying fairness in these AI systems. Our overview reveals that AI has the potential to increase the disparity in health equity among various regions of the globe without proper planning to make it equitable. We conclude by offering recommendations on the way forward, such as forming more inclusive data sets, and encouraging research teams in order to ensure AI in healthcare is produced ethically and equally benefiting all people. © 2025 IEEE.},
