	affiliations = {Department of Public and International Affairs, City University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China},
	abstract = {This chapter examines the notion of “trustworthy AI”. While various public policy documents, industry leaders, and scholars, including philosophers, use the term, on the other hand, various other philosophers have challenged the use of the term. This chapter presents challenges to the use of the term, largely based on the claim that in order for one to be trustworthy, one must be an agent and AIs are not agents. This is done by introducing a goodwill account of trust. Drawing on this account, the case is later made that while trusting an AI is conceptually possible, the agency required for having goodwill means that plausibly the current state of technology is such that “trustworthy AI” doesn’t make sense. The chapter also engages with alternative approaches to explaining the notion of trustworthy AI, including the position that adopting a stance of trust to general AI is no less reasonable than adopting a stance of trust to human persons. I argue that consideration of this position can motivate a revision in how we see trust in human relations. © 2025 The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd.},
	keywords = {Artificial intelligence; Information systems; Personnel; Public policy; 'current; Human relations; Policy documents; Philosophical aspects},
