	abstract = {This study presents the implementation and performance analysis of a medical imaging server based on Synology DSM and ARPL virtualization deployed on generic hardware within a public hospital setting. Over a continuous six-month evaluation at Dr. Gustavo Domínguez Z Hospital, the system demonstrated high operational reliability with uptime levels consistently ranging from 99.95% to 99.99% and an average availability of 99.97%. Recovery times remained below six minutes even during critical events, and no clinical data loss was recorded. The storage utilization increased from 12.1% to 18.0% during the period, reflecting a progressive integration into clinical workflows and strong user adoption. The use of Synology Hybrid RAID contributed to optimal data integrity, achieving 100% in most months and 99.99% during isolated events. Comparative analysis with other international implementations confirmed similar or superior performance. The findings support the viability of deploying non-proprietary infrastructures in healthcare environments with limited budgets while maintaining high standards of data resilience and service continuity. The study also encourages replication of this model across other institutions and long-term monitoring to assess performance under critical scenarios such as firmware updates or disaster recovery. The solution demonstrates potential to reduce costs, promote technological sovereignty, and enhance operational efficiency in clinical settings.  © 2025 IEEE.},
