	affiliations = {Amity University, Amity School of Engineering and Technology, Greater Noida Campus, Uttar Pradesh, India; Amity University, Amity School of Engineering Technology, UP, Noida, India; School of Computing Science and Engineering, Galgotias University, Uttar Pradesh, Greater Noida, India},
	abstract = {Skin cancer is one of the most prevalent and life-threatening forms of cancer worldwide, where timely and accurate diagnosis is critical for reducing mortality. However, existing deep learning-based diagnostic systems often face limitations in generalizability and fairness, as most are trained and validated on single benchmark datasets, making their clinical applicability questionable. The recently proposed SkinNetX, a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture, achieved 93.48% accuracy, 93.24% precision, 90.70% recall, and 91.82% F1-score on the ISIC 2024 dataset, outperforming over twenty state-of-the-art models including ResNet50, DenseNet121, and ViT-B/16 by an average of 4.2% in accuracy, 3.8% in precision, and 4.6% in recall. Despite these promising results, its robustness across different datasets, demographic subgroups, and imaging conditions remains unexplored, which poses a barrier to clinical acceptance. To address this gap, the present study aims to conduct a comprehensive cross-dataset and demographic validation of SkinNetX to ensure its fairness, transparency, and clinical readiness. The model will be systematically tested on widely recognized datasets such as HAM10000, BCN20000, and Derm7pt, covering diverse lesion categories and imaging sources. Furthermore, stratified performance analyses will be performed across skin tones, age groups, and genders to evaluate fairness in diagnostic outcomes. To enhance interpretability and clinician trust, explainability techniques including Grad-CAM and SHAP will be applied to highlight diagnostically relevant lesion regions, ensuring that predictions align with clinical reasoning. Preliminary findings demonstrate that SkinNetX maintains an average improvement of 5-7% in classification accuracy and F1-score across external datasets when compared to competitive models such as Swin-T, CBAM-ResNet50, and EfficientNet-B0. Importantly, demographic analysis indicates consistent diagnostic performance across both lighter and darker skin tones, suggesting reduced bias and greater equity in clinical decision-making. These results highlight that SkinNetX not only delivers superior predictive performance but also ensures fairness, robustness, and explainability key attributes required for real-world deployment in dermatology and telemedicine applications. Ultimately, this validation study provides strong evidence that SkinNetX can be considered a clinically trustworthy AI framework for automated skin cancer screening and diagnosis.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {CrossDataset Validation; Deep Learning; Demographic Fairness; Explainable AI; Skin Cancer Diagnosis},
	keywords = {Computer aided diagnosis; Decision making; Dermatology; Diseases; Learning systems; Population dynamics; Population statistics; Cancer diagnosis; Crossdataset validation; Deep learning; Demographic fairness; Diagnostic systems; Explainable AI; F1 scores; Skin cancer diagnose; Skin cancers; Skin tone; Deep learning},
