	abstract = {Nonetheless, within the Internet of Things (IoT) environment, its ever-changing, advanced, heterogeneous ecosystems continue to present unique interoperability and security predicaments. This study proposes a novel AI-driven framework to resolve IoT infrastructural fragmentality, innovatively integrating multi-agent reinforcement learning, federated anomaly detection, and semantic data unification. This framework, conceptualized from over six years of research aligned to the 2022-2025 technological landscape, interfaces decentralized AI policy implementation with lightweight encryption for devicelevel resilience and system-wide trust. An architectural defect analysis reveals cross-vendor issues plague 72 % of practical IoT deployments, and 64 % suffer from disparities of security. Out of the literature, we constructed a continuum framework that outlines the extent of the cross-domain disaffection within orchestration edge-cloud, disaffection normalization, and interface protocols. This encompassed the unaddressed areas of cross-domain-structured authentication. The proposed approach as a hybrid multi-layered architecture incorporates direct testing against practical, heterogeneous data sets from healthcare, smart cities, and industrial IoT to validate the design. We have seen experimental results show a 31.5 percent improvement in the accuracy of threat mitigation, while a 24.2 percent improvement in interoperability throughput relative to the current benchmarks. This document outlines in detail the empirically backed scalable, secure and interoperable IoT systems, describing systems with the requisite empirical depth and measurable metrics. © 2025 IEEE.},
