	affiliations = {Swinburne University of Technology, Faculty of Engineering, Computing & Science, Kuching, Malaysia},
	abstract = {This paper presents a multimodal artificial intelligence framework for construction safety management, designed to meet Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) requirements and reduce preventable injuries. The system integrates two modules: (1) a Personal Protective Equipment compliance detector combining YOLOv8 with a LoRA-fine-tuned Microsoft Florence-2 model, and (2) a text-based risk predictor using transformer adapters with ensemble evaluation. The vision module, trained on 288 images, achieved perfect helmet detection (F1 = 1.0) and high accuracy for vests (0.95), gloves (0.82), and glasses (0.75), all on CPU hardware. The text module, tested on 969 incident scenarios, showed strong recall for severe risks (0.73) and moderate performance for medium risks (0.49), though low-risk sensitivity remained limited (0.03). Limitations include slow inference on CPU and bias toward severe risks, but the framework still delivers real-time PPE monitoring and proactive incident risk analysis in a cost-effective, CPU-deployable design, advancing scalable safety management in construction. Together, the modules deliver real-time PPE monitoring and proactive incident risk analysis in a cost-effective, CPU-deployable framework, advancing scalable safety management in construction. © 2025 IEEE.},
