	abstract = {Recommender systems increasingly shape users' informational environments, influencing beliefs, preferences, and behaviors through adaptive and often opaque mechanisms. While technical and ethical safeguards have been proposed to improve system trustworthiness, they typically focus on internal properties and treat the user as a passive recipient.We argue that trust in adaptive systems - systems that not only respond to user input but also evolve their behavior based on it - is not a static attribute, but a relational process that must be supported through design. We introduce the concept of educational requirements: a subset of system-level requirements for trustworthy AI, focused on user-facing design principles that foster understanding, reflection, and control. These requirements embed epistemic support throughout the system lifecycle, enabling users to track how recommendations evolve and how their actions shape outcomes.We present echo chambers and behavioral drift as a concrete context in which educational requirements can be applied to support user awareness and epistemic resilience. In this way, educational requirements serve not only as ethical design strategies, but also as proportionate mitigation measures within the meaning of Articles 34 and 35 of the EU Digital Services Act. These articles require very large platforms to identify systemic risks and implement effective responses.In practical terms, when such risks are identified (Article 34), a platform may implement educational requirements (Article 35) not only as part of an ethical design strategy, but also as a proportionate response under the EU Digital Services Act - one that can be defended before the European Commission as both principled and compliant.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {adaptive systems; algorithmic persuasion; digital services act; educational requirements; epistemic support; explainability; human-ai interaction; recommender systems; systemic risk mitigation; trustworthy ai; user agency; user modelling},
	keywords = {Adaptive systems; Artificial intelligence; Behavioral research; Ethical aspects; Information services; Information systems; Life cycle; Requirements engineering; Algorithmic persuasion; Algorithmics; Digital service act; Digital services; Educational requirement; Epistemic support; Explainability; Human-ai interaction; Risk mitigation; Systemic risk mitigation; Systemic risks; Trustworthy ai; User agencies; User Modelling; Recommender systems},
