	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) has been radically changing the role of medical diagnostics, but there is one remaining obstacle in adopting such systems, the trust and interpretability provided by clinicians. This paper presents the concept and trial of a clinician-centered XAI system in diagnosing genetic disorders. With such interpretable machine models being combined with domain-informed visualizations and explanation accuracy statistics, the study evaluates how trust can be designed within various diagnostic contexts. Project test results on a curated test set of 2,500 patient genomic profiles show that models equipped with explanations boosted consistent assessment with clinical reference by 19.6%, and they cut the mean review time by 23%. A survey of 68 practicing clinicians indicated that 84 percent would prefer XAI outputs in the form of causal reasoning pathways over the black-box predictions. The framework also shows the existing disparities in interpretability between uncommon and more prevalent genetic diseases, which is essential information in relation to scalable deployment. These findings demonstrate that the goals of trustworthy AI in clinical genomics should be to balance accuracy, interpretability, and workflow integration allowing clinicians to trust AI-driven recommendations without losing a sense of accountability. © 2025 IEEE.},
