	abstract = {The integration of cloud-native data engineering into credit decision-making systems has transformed financial services, enabled unprecedented processing efficiency while raising significant concerns regarding algorithmic bias. As financial institutions increasingly rely on sophisticated machine learning models for credit evaluation, hidden patterns in historical data, proxy variables, and sampling biases can perpetuate discrimination against protected groups. This article examines current approaches to algorithmic transparency by reviewing technical, operational, and governance components implemented across financial institutions. The paper synthesizes best practices from existing literature on comprehensive decision logging, realtime monitoring dashboards, statistical methodologies for bias assessment, and integration with cloud-native observability stacks. Through this systematic review, we identify how financial institutions are working to ensure fair credit access while leveraging advanced analytics capabilities. We discuss documented improvements in key fairness metrics, including disparate impact ratio and equal opportunity difference across diverse demographic groups, and highlight areas where further research is needed to address remaining challenges in algorithmic fairness.  © 2025 IEEE.},
