	affiliations = {North Carolina a&t State University, Michigan, United States; Ample Inc, San Francisco, United States; University of California San Diego, Santa Clara, United States; Independent Researcher, Austin, United States; Fr Conceicao Rodrigues College of Engineering, Mumbai, India},
	abstract = {Autonomous driving systems rely heavily on artificial intelligence (AI) to perceive, plan, and act within complex environments. As these systems become more sophisticated, rigorous safety validation and verification (V&V) methods become critical to ensure reliable and trustworthy behavior under diverse and unpredictable real-world conditions. Existing standards, such as ISO 21448 (SOTIF), ISO 34502 Series, ISO/PAS 8800, and IEEE P2846, specifically address AI-driven safety through methodologies focusing on scenario-based testing, behavioral safety modeling, and AI lifecycle management. This research paper critically analyzes these four prominent standards, identifying specific gaps related to AI performance metrics, scenario comprehensiveness, real-time adaptability, and robustness testing. The paper presents targeted recommendations, including enhanced methodologies for identifying and testing edge-case scenarios (ISO 21448), AI-driven dynamic scenario updating (ISO 34502:2022 Series), integration of adversarial robustness testing (ISO/PAS 8800), and adaptive, probabilistic behavioral verification techniques (IEEE P2846). These proposed improvements provide a clear pathway toward strengthening AI safety assurance, facilitating standard harmonization, and aligning regulatory practices within the autonomous driving industry in the United States.  © 2025 IEEE.},
