	abstract = {As machine learning (ML) moves from theoretical exploration to enterprise deployment, the demand for scalable, adaptable, and interpretable models has become increasingly urgent across major industrial sectors. From healthcare diagnostics to smart manufacturing, fraud detection, agriculture, and retail personalization, scalable ML systems have demonstrated strong performance in improving efficiency, reducing costs, and enhancing predictive accuracy. This overview looks at some of the main ways machine learning systems are built and tested when they're used on a large scale. It pays special attention to modular setups, methods like federated and graph-based learning, and how well these systems can work across different industries. By combining evidences from industrial practices and academic research, this paper provides the first complete view of SMLO in a unified architectural setting, presents an integrated model, and extracts common design principles. It also takes a look at what's happening right now, like how to build AI responsibly, systems that keep learning on their own, and spreading intelligence across networks. These ideas could help shape where research and real-world use go next. © 2025 IEEE.},
