	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) has rapidly emerged as a transformative force in dentistry, offering unprecedented opportunities to support clinical decision-making in orthodontics. This review highlights the potential of AI-driven tools to enhance patient diagnosis and treatment planning. A key component of this transformation is data mining, which enables the extraction of clinically meaningful patterns from raw patient data. As these data accumulate into large-scale clinical resources, orthodontics can shift from experience-based decision-making toward a truly data-driven paradigm. Such an approach not only enhances the precision and reliability of treatment planning but also establishes the foundation for personalized and evidence-based orthodontic care. Importantly, the effective use of AI as a clinical tool requires orthodontists to understand its fundamental concepts, capabilities, and limitations. By outlining the essential knowledge and methodological considerations that clinicians should be aware of, this review offers practical guidance for integrating AI into daily orthodontic practice. Furthermore, by synthesizing current advancements and identifying future directions, it proposes a framework for building an AI environment in clinical orthodontics. The transition to data-driven strategies represents a crucial step toward more effective, reproducible, and patient-centered treatment outcomes. © 2025 Elsevier Inc.},
