	affiliations = {Graduate School of Business Sciences, University of Tsukuba, Tokyo, Japan},
	abstract = {The rapid proliferation of Digital Learning Environments, particularly Learning Management Systems (LMS), has resulted in the accumulation of vast, sensitive spatio-temporal interaction data and behavioral trajectories. This phenomenon necessitates robust governance and innovative policies for utilizing such educational big data. Leveraging this rich dataset, this paper proposes an AI utilization policy focused on enhancing Computer Adaptive Testing (CAT) through Bayesian Networks (BNs). The primary objective is to effectively optimize a learner's evolutionary path in the educational dataspace by refining the sequencing of learning items. Conventional CAT systems, primarily grounded in Item Response Theory (IRT), often present challenges regarding interpretability and do not explicitly model the causal structure or hierarchical dependencies of knowledge. This limitation potentially impacts the system's prescriptive capabilities, treating the learning process as a black box. Our approach leverages BNs to explicitly model the complex, conditional dependency structure among test questions based on real learner data collected from university SPI test preparation courses over two academic years (2022-2023, N=1000 students). The resulting BN structure serves as a verifiable, standardized Behavioral Dependency Model that inherently enhances system utility through transparency and facilitates standardization across educational platforms, thereby enabling 'Human Life Evolution' through optimized learning paths. We analyze historical data to identify systematic discrepancies between manual difficulty settings and empirically-derived learning dependencies, revealing gaps as large as 5 levels between strongly connected concepts. Furthermore, we detail the experimental design for the 2024 academic year (N=298), where this data-driven approach will be validated through controlled comparison against traditional methods. This research validates the design of explainable probabilistic models as essential components of a trustworthy educational dataspace for behavioral insight and personalized learning optimization. © 2025 IEEE.},
