	abstract = {This paper is a proposal of an AI-based energy optimization framework in smart building systems conducting supervised and reinforcement-based learning and explainable AI. The models were trained and assessed based on the data from Kaggle Building Energy Use Dataset that includes 19735 samples. The forecasting accuracy of LSTM network was the highest with RMSE of 2.84 kWh and R2 of 0.93 as compared to the Random Forest and XGBoost regressor. To optimise the control, the Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) agent minimised the mean energy consumption by 18.3% and had 94% compliance with thermal comfort as compared to 15.1% of Deep Q-Network (DQN). SHAP analysis has shown that the main factors affecting model choices were the outdoor temperatures, occupancy density, and the time of the day. In comparison to the traditional rule-based control, AI strategy was 11% more efficient concerning the overall load stabilizing and it proved to be highly adaptive in the changing conditions. The results attest to the fact that predictive and adaptive AI models are essential to improve energy efficiency and operating visibility considerably and have become a scalable solution to sustainable energy management in the present-day buildings. © 2025 IEEE.},
