	affiliations = {Division of Molecular Biology, School of Life Sciences, JSS Academy of Higher Education and Research, Karnataka, Mysuru, India},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) is essential to healthcare because it helps with patient diagnosis, medication discovery and development, better patient-physician communication, and medical record transcription. The two key pillars of healthcare are explainable and responsive AI (XAI and RAI), which enable morally and transparently sound decision-making. They may alter the way healthcare is provided by increasing the quantity and scalability of healthcare workers. AI algorithms are frequently more cost-effective and accurate than human physicians when it comes to analyzing medical imaging data to find early signs of illnesses like cancer. XAI techniques clarify complex model predictions and highlight important features of biological datasets. The ethical use of AI is simultaneously guided by RAI principles, which ensure equity, rectify biases, and encourage openness throughout the model life cycle. The responsible interpretation of patient outcomes made possible by the combination of XAI and RAI promotes accountability and trust. In order to facilitate responsible AI deployment in biomedical contexts, regulatory frameworks must be adjusted and model complexity and interpretability must be balanced, among other issues addressed in this analysis of present issues and potential future developments. Apart from that, the overall performance and reliability of AI models in biological applications and healthcare analysis are covered in this chapter, along with ongoing research to improve the explainability and accountability of deep learning models. This chapter also covers the techniques used in XAI and RAI and its applications to data analytics, biomedical image processing, and classification. © 2025 Scrivener Publishing LLC.},
