	abstract = {Because of service-based architecture (SBA), network slicing, edge computing (MEC), and the enormous Internet of Things (IoT), 5G networks will have a very large attack surface. The established security protection that is inflexible with respect to the scale and dynamism of the 5G network cannot match this. This study aims to create an artificial intelligence-based security framework that integrates federated learning (FL), Graph neural networks (GNNs), time-series deep learning and reinforcement learning (DRL) into 5G networks based on both control planes and user planes. Constant risk evaluation, anomaly-detecting, and proactive mitigation are all capabilities that are performed by the framework across the RAN, transport, and 5G Core fabric. We offer a formal definition of a 5G threat model, consider the architecture and algorithms of the system, and outline an implementation blueprint where we use cloud-native 5G cores and an open RAN stack. To complete the evaluation plans, both synthetic and real traffic traces will be used in closed-loop experiments. These experiments will show an increased detection F1-score of 0.95 or greater, a 40-70 percent reduction in mean time to detect (MTTD), and a less than one percent latency rise on average eMBB/URLLC network layers. In this paper. The limitations and how to build a reliable and trustworthy AI that will lead to 6G are discussed. © 2025 IEEE.},
