	abstract = {The SARI Competition is a strategic educational initiative led by the Saudi Space Agency in collaboration with the Brazilian company Ideia Space and with institutional support from the Brazilian Space Agency. Aimed at Saudi university students aged 18 to 27, the competition's main goal is to foster technical training in space engineering through the design, construction, and future operation of nanosatellites. Structured into five progressive phases, SARI guided participants from the development of a Mission Design Review (MDR) to the final construction of two functional orbital satellites, with launch planned for the program’s final stage. The curriculum adopted active learning methodologies-such as Project-Based Learning (PBL), flipped classroom, and Design Thinking-and promoted an interdisciplinary learning environment focused on solving real-world aerospace challenges. Activities included synchronous and asynchronous classes, in-person workshops, hands-on testing with educational satellites, collaborative projects, and evaluative presentations. The technical content covered nanosatellite subsystems, embedded programming, electronics, instrumentation, the space environment, and mission communications, all applied to practical challenges with a strong emphasis on innovation, teamwork, and scientific thinking. The international partnership not only ensured knowledge transfer between Brazil and Saudi Arabia but also established a multicultural and technically robust educational platform. Notably, female participation increased during the final stages of the competition, and continuous project-based assessments allowed for in-depth evaluation of group performance and learning. The SARI Competition demonstrates the effectiveness of international frameworks in training a new generation of professionals for the Saudi space industry. It represents a scalable and replicable model of educational cooperation for emerging countries seeking to strengthen scientif