	affiliations = {Canterbury Christ Church University, Computing, AI and Cyber security, Canterbury, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) is transforming Ambient Assisted Living (AAL) by enabling real-time monitoring, predictive health assessment, and personalised support. However, the adoption of AI in AAL is hindered by lack of transparency, potential biases, and limited interpretability. This paper introduces M<sup>3</sup> X-AAL-a Multimodal, Multi-tier, Meta-explainability framework designed to enhance trust and understanding in AI-powered AAL systems. The framework integrates diverse data modalities (numerical, textual, and visual) and provides explanations at three hierarchical levels: Tier 1 delivers local and global insights specific to each modality; Tier 2 contextualises these explanations with metadata to identify biases and subgroupspecific patterns; and Tier 3 monitors temporal evolution to detect concept drift and maintain fairness over time. By combining SHAP, word cloud analysis, and Grad-CAM visualisations, M<sup>3</sup> X-AAL offers comprehensive interpretability for technical experts, domain specialists, and end-users alike. Experimental results demonstrate the framework's ability to improve transparency, fairness, and trustworthiness in safety-critical AAL environments. © 2025 IEEE.},
