	affiliations = {Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, Saveetha School of Engineering, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, India; Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, Saveetha School of Engineering, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, India},
	abstract = {The conventional clinical trials are frequently characterized by a lack of personalization, low levels of data reliability and low levels of interpretation, which restrict their use in real-life. The present paper suggests a personalized clinical trial model (DTOC) that incorporates organic synthesis-based drug modeling, multi-omics data fusion, and interpretable artificial intelligence (XAI)-based reliability pipelines with the help of digital twins. Physiology of every patient is simulated as a digital twin and provides an opportunity to simulate genomic, clinical, and quality-of-life signs in real-time. The organic synthesis module is the approximation of the drug-patient interaction on the molecular level, and the XAI layer increases the transparency and compliance with the requirements of GDPR and IEEE principles on AI ethics. Experimental validation with 500 patient datasets showed that it gained significant performance improvement, obtaining 92.3% and XAI reliability of 0.89 and improved in the reliability index by 16% over the baseline deep-learning-based methods. The suggested DTOC framework would guarantee reproducible, interpretable, and ethically consistent trial outcomes. Through the combination of customized drug production and explainable digital health simulation, this study enhances the future of customized clinical trials that can hasten the process of drug discovery, enhance health outcomes, and increase confidence in the AI-driven healthcare systems.  © 2025 IEEE.},
