	affiliations = {Singapore Institute of Management Global Education, Singapore},
	abstract = {Interdisciplinary discourse on the Anthropocene and sustainability education has intensified in recent years as we navigate the challenges of this present geological epoch. Concurrently, the attention and debates concerning the emerging impacts of artificial intelligence (AI) on industry, society and education have been vigorous. However, there have been divergences in its definition, conceptualisation and application contexts across various disciplines and within the higher education space. Due to its nascent yet quicksilver nature, achieving consensus on the operationalisation, policy implications and ethical responsibilities of AI in higher education has been challenging, let alone the debates surrounding AI for Sustainability Education (AI4SE), sustainable development and ethics. Therefore, there is a need to critically examine AI's evolution, concerns and implementation within sustainability education and its associated Sustainable Development Goals. This chapter is conceptual and aims to examine the intersection of AI, ethics and the anthropocenic turn in sustainability paradigms within teaching and learning. A proposed conceptual model of propositions for AI4SE is also presented for further empirical research. © The Editors and Contributing Authors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.},
