	affiliations = {Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, Saveetha School of Law, Chennai, 77, India},
	abstract = {The JusticeComp-Net framework is designed to evaluate the Victim Compensation Scheme, using a hybrid ensemble learning model. By integrating structured judicial records and unstructured court transcripts, the system leverages transformer-based NLP models, gradient boosting, and meta-classifier stacking to predict compensation outcomes accurately. The model underwent rigorous evaluation using various machine learning techniques, including ResNet-152, XGBoost, CatBoost, and Gradient Boosting, ensuring high reliability in legal case predictions. The results demonstrate that JusticeComp-Net achieves an accuracy of 96.88%, significantly outperforming traditional machine learning approaches. Key performance indicators such as F1-score (96.1%), Precision (95.7%), and Recall (96.5%) validate the effectiveness of the model. Additionally, SHAP-based explainability ensures legal transparency in compensation prediction. Sentiment analysis using LSTM with attention mechanisms further refines verdict inclination assessments. The findings reveal compensation disparities based on case types, highlighting areas for legal reform. This research contributes to the intersection of law and AI, facilitating fairer compensation distribution. Future extensions may include real-time legal database integration and policy-oriented AI frameworks to enhance victim support mechanisms.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Gradient Boosting; Hybrid Ensemble Learning; JusticeComp-Net; Legal AI; SHAP Analysis; Transformer-Based NLP; Victim Compensation Scheme},
