	affiliations = {GA, United States; Cvr College of Engineering, Telangana, Hyderabad, India},
	abstract = {The rapid proliferation of Internet of Things devices has led to a surge in real-time data generation, requiring efficient and accurate anomaly detection mechanisms, especially in resource-constrained edge environments. Traditional centralized methods often suffer from high latency, excessive bandwidth usage, and privacy concerns, making them less suitable for modern IoT deployments. This paper proposes a novel bio-inspired Edge AI framework designed for real-time anomaly detection in distributed IoT sensor networks. Drawing inspiration from biological systems such as the human immune response, the framework integrates adaptive temporal feature extraction with an Artificial Immune System based anomaly detection module. It is optimized for lightweight, decentralized processing to support low-power edge devices. The system architecture includes preprocessing, bio-inspired feature computation, self-organizing anomaly classification, and efficient communication modules. Comparative evaluations against baseline algorithms Support Vector Machine and Isolation Forest demonstrate the superiority of the proposed model in terms of detection accuracy (94.6%), reduced latency (28 ms), and lower energy consumption (15 mJ). These results confirm the framework's effectiveness for real-time deployment in dynamic IoT environments. The proposed approach enables scalable, energy-efficient, and privacy preserving anomaly detection, paving the way for next-generation edge intelligence in mission-critical applications such as smart grids, healthcare, and industrial monitoring  © 2025 IEEE.},
