	affiliations = {Washington University of Science and Technology, United States; Virginia University of Science and Technology Virginia, United States; University of Ulster, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {The growth of digital security incidents and their complexity and volume require quick and compliant response system. Conventional automated systems have typically focused on speed at the expense of transparency, which poses some problems to organizations facing tough regulatory and governance rules. In this paper, an Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) framework of compliance-based security incident response is proposed, in which the decision transparency is interwoven into the detection, triage, and mitigation workflow. The combination of interpretable machine learning models with rule-based compliance policies, not only speeds up incident resolution but also provides human-readable explanation as per legal and industry standards. The proposed solution takes advantage of the explainability methods including feature attribution and causal reasoning by justifying incident prioritization and response activities hence building the trust among security analysts, auditors, and regulators. Experimental tests of the system on real-world cybersecurity data show that the XAI-enabled system has competitive detection rates with low false positives and generates actionable and compliance-sensitive insights. This piece underscores the importance of explainability in mitigating the disconnect between AI-based automation and compliance-based security governance, which will eventually lead to a resilient and auditable incident response strategies. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Compliance; Cybersecurity; Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI); Interpretability; Machine Learning; Regulatory Governance; Security Incident Response; Trustworthy AI},
