	journal = {AI-Driven Digital Transformation: Perspectives from a Business School},
	pages = {249 - 262},
	doi = {10.4324/9781003659143-25},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105024174769?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Sociology and Business Ethics, Poznań University of Economics and Business, Poland; Linköping University, Sweden},
	abstract = {This chapter explores how the Regulation for Artificial Intelligence (AI Act) sets the tone for the normative and institutional direction taken by the European Union – effectively “calling the tune” for how AI systems should be governed. Drawing on institutional theory and Max Weber’s concepts of value and instrumental rationality, the authors conducted a value-oriented thematic analysis. As such, it uncovers an axiologically informed map that elicits the ideals expressed through the regulatory rhetoric in recitals, which precede the legally binding provisions. The study employed AI-assisted qualitative analysis, which enabled the tracing of values based on linguistic clues in the documents used to justify the proposed regulation. The authors categorized these values into four groups: foundational (e.g., dignity and freedom), substantive (e.g., safety and prosperity), instrumental per se (e.g., risk and innovation), and epistemic (e.g., transparency and accountability). The findings show a common harmony in the identified values related to the basic societal fabric that must be preserved, and what AI systems must be in order not to corrupt it. However, they also reveal a tension between two regulatory overtones: a cautious one focused on addressing current concerns and a more aspirational one promoting AI-mediated change. The dominant tone leans toward restraint, suggesting a “two steps back, one step forward” approach. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Witold Abramowicz, Marek Kowalkiewicz and Krzysztof Węcel; individual chapters, the contributors.},
	keywords = {'current; AI systems; European union; Four-group; Institutional theory; Instrumental rationalities; Qualitative analysis; Thematic analysis; Value-based; Artificial intelligence},
