	year = {2025},
	journal = {Proceedings - 2025 IEEE 7th International Conference on Trust, Privacy and Security in Intelligent Systems, and Applications, TPS-ISA 2025},
	pages = {703 - 716},
	doi = {10.1109/TPS-ISA67132.2025.00087},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036500795?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {University of the Cumberlands, United States},
	abstract = {Enterprise compliance and audit teams face high demand for automation due to regulatory complexity, scale requirements, and resource optimization due to budgetary constraints. This study evaluates four leading Large Language Models (LLMs): GPT-4o, Claude 3, Gemini 2.5 Flash Pro, and Meta LLaMA-4 for automated compliance control testing in three universal IT audit scenarios such as User Access Review, Backup and Restore Testing, and Change Management validation. The research employed realistic mock audit datasets containing 1,050 total records with systematically embedded compliance exceptions. Each of the LLMs was tested across the 3 controls to perform the audit on the data provided. A comprehensive trustworthiness assessment framework was developed to measure accuracy, output quality, processing efficiency, and hallucination rates as key metrics for determining appropriate levels of human-AI collaboration. Statistical analysis using Cohen's d effect sizes and analysis of variance confirmed significant performance variations between the models and control types. Claude 3 achieved the highest overall accuracy (83.27% across control types) with very large effect sizes compared to all competitors (Cohen's d >1.50). GPT-4o showed optimal processing efficiency (11.67 seconds average) with zero observed hallucinations across all test scenarios, representing perfect reliability for factual consistency. The experiments conducted demonstrate that task complexity in addition to model architecture, determines appropriate human-AI collaboration levels. Systematic operational controls achieved 70.43% average accuracy suitable for high AI autonomy with minimal human oversight (Tier 1 teaming), while complex procedural controls averaged only 37.46% accuracy, requiring human-led collaboration with AI assistance (Tier 3 teaming). The findings suggest that LLMs can be used effectively to augment specific audit processes by automating the data analysis but require human validation of the results produced to ensure completeness and accuracy of the analysis. This research provides evidence-based guidance on strength's and weaknesses of various LLM's data analysis capabilities when provided IT audit related tasks while identifying critical limitations to be considered for regulatory compliance use cases. This research also advances human-AI teamwork by establishing quantitative trustworthiness thresholds, validated metrics for ongoing reliability assessment, and a risk-calibrated collaboration framework based on optimal AI autonomy levels.  © 2025 IEEE.},
