	affiliations = {Gebze Technical University, Dept of Computer Engineering, Kocaeli, Gebze, Turkey; HAVELSAN Inc., Naval Combat Mgmt. Tech. Center, Pendik, Istanbul, Turkey; Gebze Technical University, Dept of Data Science and Analytics, Kocaeli, Gebze, Turkey},
	abstract = {Predicting the cycle-to-cycle State of Health (SOH) of lithium-ion batteries is essential for ensuring the reliability and safety of modern energy storage systems. In this work, we introduce an explainable AI framework that leverages the newly released NASA 2023 accelerated life-testing dataset - comprising 9,613 discharge cycles across 26 battery packs under constant, variable, and second-life conditions - to deliver both high fidelity and transparency in SOH estimation. First, we construct a sophisticated feature-engineering pipeline that transforms high-frequency voltage, current, and temperature measurements into cycle-level and rolling-window statistics. We then benchmark traditional machine learning models (XGBoost, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, and Linear Regression) against five t emporal d eep-learning architectures (including LSTM, BiLSTM, Multi-Head LSTM, LSTM with Attention, and a CNN-LSTM hybrid). Our CNN-LSTM model attains state-of-the-art performance with an R2 of 0.977 and an MAE of 0.42, while XGBoost achieves competitive accuracy (R2 = 0.888) and, when paired with SHAP analysis, provides detailed global and local feature-importance insights. We demonstrate that our framework generalizes robustly across diverse operational profiles, striking an effective balance between predictive power and interpretability. This dual-track approach establishes new SOH-prediction benchmarks for industrial predictive-maintenance applications and paves the way for transparent, data-driven battery management.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Battery Management; Explainable AI; LSTM; Predictive Maintenance; SHAP; State of Health; Time Series Analysis},
