	affiliations = {Al Ain University, College of Law, Al Ain, United Arab Emirates; Abu Dhabi Police, United Arab Emirates; Tanta university, Faculty of law, Tanta, Egypt},
	abstract = {With the advancement of technology, Artificial Intelligence has become a significant tool in all fields, including in criminal procedures, being utilized in facial recognition, video analysis, crime prediction, and tracking individuals' movements. However, this extensive use poses risks of privacy violations, necessitating stringent legal safeguards to ensure protection. This study aims to explore the impact of using AI in criminal proceedings on the protection of privacy and individual rights. It addresses the legal and ethical challenges arising from the use of such technologies in the collection and analysis of personal data during criminal investigations. The research concludes that AI represents a powerful instrument for enhancing the effectiveness of criminal investigations due to its capacity to process large volumes of data with speed and accuracy. Nevertheless, the use of these technologies raises concerns regarding privacy protection and the safeguarding of individuals' rights, particularly when personal data is collected without explicit consent. The study further highlights the need to establish a legal framework governing the use of AI in this context, ensuring the protection of individual rights and shielding them from potential violations resulting from the use of such technologies. The study recommends the development of comprehensive legislation in the United Arab Emirates to regulate the use of AI technologies in criminal investigations, ensuring transparency and prior consent from individuals before collecting their data. It also stresses the importance of maintaining human oversight over AI systems to prevent automated analysis from infringing upon the right to a fair trial.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Artificial Intelligence; Criminal Proceedings; Criminal Protection; Personal Data; Privacy},
