	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036498982?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Pondicherry University, Department of Computer Science, Pondicherry, India},
	abstract = {Deep learning based automated retinal disease diagnosis has become an effective tool for early diagnosis of diseases like glaucoma, cataracts, and diabetic retinopathy (DR). As machine learning methods become more common in clinical processes, the challenge of machine unlearning arises. This involves removing specific patient data from trained models while maintaining their predicted performance. Following privacy laws, such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), adds to this difficulty. This work uses the ODIR-5K dataset to build and evaluate a classification framework based on Vision Transformer (ViT) that can selectively unlearn. The study separates instances of DR, cataract, and glaucoma using an unlearning procedure that removes specific samples and re-trains the model. Along with visual comparisons of heatmap focus before and after unlearning, both explanation-based metrics - XAI Forgetting Score (XFS) and cosine similarity - were evaluated, as well as the Intersection over Union (IoU) of Grad-CAM heatmaps and conventional classification metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and AUC-ROC). The findings indicate that the proposed approach can "forget"specific samples while still demonstrating a strong capacity to categorize - the model showed a macro-averaged F1-score of 0.75 and an Accuracy of 0.92. The study indicates that privacy-preserving AI in ophthalmology is feasible and retains diagnostic accuracy. The proposed method combines interpretability with unlearning to promote clinical trustworthiness through regulatory compliance, thereby alleviating a vital tension that currently limits the use of medical AI. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Explainable AI (XAI); Grad-CAM; Machine Unlearning; ODIR-5K Dataset; Retinal Disease Classification; Vision Transformer (ViT)},
