	abstract = {Glioma grading remains a critical challenge in neuro-oncology, requiring accurate distinction between low-grade gliomas (LGG) and glioblastoma multiforme (GBM) for effective treatment planning. This paper proposes a multimodal transformer-based framework that integrates MRI scans, histopathology, and genomic biomarkers to enhance diagnostic accuracy. The architecture combines Vision Transformer, Swin Transformer, and EfficientNetV2 with a genomic transformer encoder, followed by cross-attention-based fusion and a classification head. Quantitative evaluations using confusion matrices, ROC and precision-recall curves, and cross-validation analyses demonstrate superior performance compared to unimodal baselines, with consistent improvements in accuracy, F1-score, sensitivity, and specificity. Furthermore, explainability tools such as Grad-CAM and attention rollout provide interpretability, reinforcing the clinical relevance of the framework. The results confirm that multimodal fusion not only improves prediction robustness but also paves the way for clinically trustworthy AI systems in glioma diagnosis.  © 2025 IEEE.},
