	affiliations = {Law & Forensics, United States; FTI Consulting, United States},
	abstract = {The history of forensic science has seen a significant evolution, from ancient techniques of physical examination to modern advancements enhanced by digital tools and artificial intelligence (AI). This chapter explores the transformation of forensic practices, beginning with early autopsies and the development of pathology, through to the recent incorporation of AI in digital and network forensics, language analysis, and forensic medicine. AI has enabled forensic investigators to analyze vast amounts of data rapidly, identify patterns, detect deepfakes, and categorize evidence with greater efficiency and accuracy. Tools like CLIPr AI, Reality Defender, and Magnet Copilot exemplify AI's role in simplifying evidence analysis, while network monitoring platforms such as Datadog enhance security by detecting anomalies in real-time. In forensic medicine, AI assists in identifying traumatic injuries and conducting odonto-stomatological examinations with increased precision. However, the adoption of AI is not without challenges, including issues of bias, explainability, and overfitting, which can hinder its reliability in complex, real-world scenarios. The chapter concludes by emphasizing the need for collaboration between policymakers and technology experts to create regulatory frameworks that ensure AI's responsible use in forensic science, enhancing investigative procedures while safeguarding ethical and societal standards. © The Editors and Contributors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.},
