	abstract = {The emergence of intelligent learning platforms founded on intelligent communications has transformed the digital learning ecosystem in a revolutionary manner. While these systems provide adaptive, interactive, and AI-driven learning environments, they raise some significant concerns regarding safeguarding children's personal data. UNICEF (2023) estimates that over 70% of children use digital learning technologies, which, without age-appropriate safeguards, increases exposure to opaque data collection and profiling risks. Still, most legal systems lack any relevant law to protect their digital identity, privacy, and behavioural data. This paper has explored how children's data are gathered, processed, and potentially exploited in smart learning environments that employ smart systems such as AI-based tutors, chatbots, and real-time analytics software. The aim is to identify privacy risks and recommend protection measures specific to children's needs. Through a doctrinal research approach, the study compares international data protection frameworks (e.g., GDPR, OECD Guidelines), UNICEF's policymaking framework, and policymaking in online learning. By comparison, it addresses platform-specific risks like stealth profiling, intrusive in-home data collections, and data exploitation. Findings are that the majority of platforms have ambiguous or poorly defined consent practices and low levels of transparency, frequently avoiding parental control and ethical design. The study concludes by recommending privacy-by-design practices, AI audit trails, and age-appropriate consent mechanisms to protect children's more secure digital classroom lives. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Children's Data Privacy; Consent Mechanisms; Digital Risk Governance; Intelligent Systems; Smart Education},
	keywords = {Computer aided instruction; Data communication systems; Data privacy; E-learning; Education computing; Engineering education; Learning systems; Teaching; Child data privacy; Consent mechanism; Data collection; Digital risk governance; Intelligent communication; Learning environments; Policy making; Privacy risks; Risk governance; Smart education; Intelligent systems},
