	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) was designed to automate cognition. It is widely used in the cultural milieu, influencing our choices, behavior and imagination. In order to function, AI algorithms need to be fed by thousands of pieces of data, generally available on the Internet. AI encompasses a range of technological practices, such as Symbolic AI, which relies on human-created symbology such as language to create rules that can then be reproduced, or Machine Learning, which involves computational systems learning structures from data, with the aim of making predictions or decisions. The new role of AI in the creative process, particularly in automation, sketch generation and experimentation, is indisputable, but the place of the artist remains crucial. In 2019, UNESCO's General Conference adopted 40 C/Resolution 37, mandating the organization to draw up an international standard-setting instrument on the ethics of AI in the form of a recommendation. © ISTE Ltd 2025.},
