	abstract = {Energy-intensive building materials, such as cement and steel, contribute significantly to carbon emissions in the construction industry. As a means of mitigating climate impacts while promoting circular economy practices, carbon-negative construction has gained popularity in recent years. This paper examines innovations in recycled and waste-derived materials, including supplementary cementitious materials, mine tailings, biochar, construction and demolition waste, and industrial by-products, to improve sustainability and sequester carbon. Furthermore, the study explores the integration of nanotechnology, life cycle assessment frameworks, and hybrid systems designed to enhance both environmental and mechanical efficiency. Major challenges identified include the diversity of feedstocks, limited standardisation, and inadequate validation of durability. Future directions include hybrid material systems, digital tools, artificial intelligence, policy frameworks supporting circular economies, and large-scale demonstrations. For the construction industry to achieve carbon-neutral and carbon-negative pathways, innovative recycling strategies are critical.  © Published under licence by IOP Publishing Ltd},
