	affiliations = {University of Piraeus, Department of Informatics, Piraeus, Greece},
	abstract = {This paper introduces a framework in Requirements Engineering for mitigating bias in user modelling resulting from clustering and stereotype-based inference. Methods that group users by similarity or apply rule-based assumptions are commonly used during system initialization but may lead to persistent misrepresentation of users and reduced fairness. The proposed approach prioritizes individualization, whereby user models are constructed and updated based on observed behavior rather than demographic attributes or peer similarity. The framework is specified through a set of use cases, actor-system interaction definitions, and functional requirements that enable the systematic replacement of initial assumptions with behaviorinformed representations. It supports modular system design by separating user model construction from content or advice generation. While the approach is demonstrated in personalization contexts, the principles are applicable to any system that uses user modelling to guide automated outputs. By incorporating fairness constraints at the specification level, this work supports the development of more accurate and equitable interactive systems.  © 2025 IEEE.},
