	abstract = {The rapid deployment of artificial intelligence (AI) systems across India's digital ecosystem has fundamentally transformed personal data processing mechanisms, creating unprecedented challenges for privacy protection and regulatory compliance. This paper presents a comprehensive analysis of AI's impact on digital personal data in India, examining citizen awareness levels and evaluating the adequacy of existing statutory guidance frameworks. Through the analysis of regulations, surveys conducted among citizens, and the comparison of policies, we identified that there are obvious gaps in the people awareness and in the legal frameworks that govern the AI-based data processing. Our research indicates that very less percent of population in India that utilizes the internet has knowledge of the actions of algorithms on their data. The existing legislations lack specific guidelines on automated decision-making systems based on artificial intelligence. There should be a multi-tier regulatory system that combines a risk-based regulation of the AI governing body with mandatory algorithmic auditing and increased transparency regulations. The present paper highlights the current state of understanding regarding AI governance issues in emerging economies by describing the key challenges and proposing policy for adoption by technology practitioners and policymakers. © 2025 IEEE.},
