	affiliations = {PSNA College of Engineering and Technology, Dept. of AI and DS, Dindigul, India; Karpagam Institute of Technology, Dept. of Artificial Intelligence and Data Science, Coimbatore, India; KGiSL Institute of Technology, Dept. of IT, Coimbatore, India; SNS College of Technology, Department of Mechanical Engineering, Comibatore, India},
	abstract = {Generative AI has revolutionised phishing and other cybercrimes, enabling contextually adaptive, polymorphic, and human-like attacks. These sophisticated methods allow even amateur phishers to bypass rule-based and signature-based defences, facilitating large-scale phishing operations. To ethically study this phenomenon, we developed a simulation framework featuring a synthetic testbed, non-operational placeholders, and consenting participants. Our analysis revealed that generative AI-enabled phishing exhibits higher perceived authenticity compared to traditional template-based methods. To address these challenges, we introduced a Defensive AI framework incorporating language feature analysis, provenance tracking, and behavioural anomaly detection. In addition to ethical controls and explainable AI, the framework includes adaptive protection, blind spot detection, anomaly detection, adaptive awareness, and responsible experimentation. Generative AI elevates phishing to an intelligent adversarial campaign, necessitating advanced AI-driven countermeasures focused on explainable AI, adaptive awareness training, and responsible experimentation. This framework has significant implications for ethics governance, adaptive protection, and provenance-aware detection mechanisms. © 2025 IEEE.},
