	title = {Fostering Student Responsibility and Sustainability Literacy Through AI-Based Learning Tools},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {2025 5th International Conference on Educational Communications and Technology, ICTAECT 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ICTAECT67351.2025.11391256},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035490780?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {FPT University, Business Administration Department, Can Tho City, Viet Nam; FPT University, Entrepreneurship Department, Can Tho City, Viet Nam},
	abstract = {This research examines how learning with AI technologies and instructional conditions affect Students' Sustainability Responsibility (SSR) in higher education settings. Based on quantitative data collected from 249 undergraduate students at FPT University, Can Tho Campus, the study applies multiple regression and moderation testing using PROCESS Model 1 to analyze both direct and interaction impacts of AI Learning Tools (AILT), AI Ethics Awareness (AIEA), Learning Engagement (LE), Educational Environment (EE), and Sustainability Literacy (SL). Results indicate that AILT, LE, and EE significantly enhance SSR, emphasizing the role of active learner involvement, supportive educational settings, and AI-enabled tools in fostering responsible and sustainability-oriented behavior. Conversely, AIEA and SL do not present a significant direct influence on SSR, suggesting that ethical awareness and sustainability knowledge alone may be insufficient to trigger behavioral change without contextual or practical reinforcement. The moderation findings show that SL weakens the effects of both AIEA and LE on SSR. This outcome suggests that students with higher sustainability literacy tend to demonstrate responsibility through internal motivation rather than depending on external encouragement or ethical prompts. Theoretically, this study contributes by integrating AI-enabled learning, ethics, and sustainability literacy into a comprehensive analytical model. From a practical perspective, it recommends that universities develop AI-augmented educational environments that prioritize learner engagement, contextual guidance, and early integration of sustainability training. Such an approach can nurture intrinsic responsibility and equip learners to manage ethical challenges in AI-driven educational contexts, while also supporting broader sustainability objectives.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Artificial Intelligence; Educational Environment; Learning Engagement; Sustainability Literacy; Sustainability Responsibility},
