	affiliations = {RHET AI Center/Institute for Media Studies, University of Tübingen, Tübingen, Germany},
	abstract = {The paper explores the impact of Artificial Intelligence (AI) on global orders by asking to what extent current practices of AI development, production, and use can be considered globally just. It adopts an infrastructural perspective, addressing both the infrastructures on which AI is built, and AI as a (particularly powerful) infrastructure, capable of transforming almost every sphere of life, as well as traditional infrastructures that used to function without AI. Drawing on insights from decolonial theory, Global South Studies, and Critical Technology Studies, as well as current examples, the article reveals how AI and related infrastructures create and reinforce colonial and patriarchal orders and transform social structures to the detriment of minorities and vulnerable groups. By demonstrating that those who benefit most from AI are not the same as those who are most affected by risk and harm, the paper argues that AI and related infrastructures in their current form cannot be considered globally just, and thus contribute to structural inequality with ordering effects on a global scale. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2025.},
