	abstract = {The three fundamental principles of information security - Confidentiality, Integrity and Availability (CIA) - have long been regarded as the core framework in traditional ICT environments. However, the rapid advancement and widespread adoption of Artificial Intelligence (AI) have introduced novel security threats that cannot be sufficiently addressed by the CIA triad alone. AI-specific vulnerabilities such as prompt injection, data poisoning, model extraction and adversarial sample attacks pose significant risks to confidentiality, integrity, availability across data, models and infrastructures. To address these challenges, this study proposes an extended security framework, CIA+, which builds upon the traditional CIA principles. CIA+ incorporates four additional principles - Transparency, Accountability, Robustness and Ethics - resulting in a total of seven elements. This research analyzes the definition and implementation strategies for each principle and presents a framework applicable to practical domains, such as medical AI. Furthermore, the study emphasizes the necessity of prototyping the CIA+-based security framework, conducting benchmark validations across diverse industries and exploring institutional alignment with international standardization bodies. © 2025 IEEE.},
