	affiliations = {BV(DU)COE, Pune, Department of Computer Engineering, Pune, India; BV(DU)COE, Pune, Department of Computer Science and Business System, Pune, India},
	abstract = {Disability in infants (high-risk infants) represents a cognitive and adaptive impairment emerging in early development, often emerging in early childhood. Early identification, especially in high-risk neonates, is vital for timely intervention, yet current clinical and unimodal computational methods remain subjective or limited in accuracy. This paper presents a hybrid AI-driven framework for early ID risk prediction that integrates structural MRI, functional EEG, and clinical parameters. A two-stage fusion strategy is introduced feature-level integration of neuroimaging and electrophysiological biomarkers, followed by decision-level ensemble modeling. Experimental evaluation using multiple public neonatal datasets demonstrates consistent improvements of 12-15% over single-modality baselines. The proposed CNN-LSTM with Random Forest achieved 90.2% accuracy and 0.94 AUC, surpassing existing methods. The key contribution of this work lies in establishing the first multimodal decision-support framework for neonatal ID risk detection, offering enhanced robustness, sensitivity, and generalizability compared to unimodal and partially multimodal approaches. © 2025 IEEE.},
