	abstract = {The 5G, 6G, and IoT have resulted in modern wireless networks that are complex and non-linear in interactions between cross-layer parameters that could not be sufficiently captured in traditional analytical models. This paper discusses these issues through the lens of unsupervised and supervised learning in the form of a data-driven AI framework. Exploratory Data Analysis showed weak linear relationships, which led to applying PCA and the K-Means clustering to identify three different states of operation networks. We subsequently analyze several regression models in the throughput prediction, with the results indicating that the traditional models, such as Linear Regression and SVR, do not provide strong results (RMSE 11, R<sup>2</sup> 0.76-0.84). On the contrary, more sophisticated ensemble techniques, such as Random Forest, Histogram-based Gradient Boosting, and Stacking, perform almost perfectly (R<sup>2</sup> =1.000, RMSE 0.028). Our findings prove that AI-based ensemble models are better at predicting network performance accurately and proactively to facilitate smart and proactive governance of resources.  © 2025 IEEE.},
