	abstract = {Large Language Models (LLMs) including GPT, LLaMA, and PaLM have been shown to be capable of transformations in fields like healthcare to finance. Nonetheless, the expensive nature of the computational and financial training, deployment, and scaling of these models continue to be a major impediment to practical implementation. The given paper research examines cost-efficiency frameworks in scaling LLMs by focusing on model optimization methods, hardware- conscious deployment models, and resource allocation sustainability. We suggest to compare the methods of cost- reduction such as model compression, parameter-efficient fine- tuning, distributed training, and inference optimization. A performance/scalability/cost balance framework is presented, which allows the usage of LLMs by enterprises and research institutions without affecting accuracy. Experimental performance as well as case studies show that near state of the art performance can be obtained with up to 40% reduction of computational overheads by adopting hybrid strategies. The research paper is part of the development of sustainable and economically feasible AI ecosystems. © 2025 IEEE.},
