	affiliations = {Centurion University of Technology and Management, India; Sardar Patel University, India; Panimalar Engineering College, India; Dhanalakshmi Srinivasan College of Engineering and Technology, India; Sri Sri University, India; School of Computing, SRM Institute of Science and Technology, India; Saveetha School of Engineering, Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, India},
	abstract = {AI technologies have advanced over the recent years at a very fast pace, and the automotive manu facturing industries have not been left behind on this. Automated safety innovations, including machine vision systems, robotics, and predictive maintenance (Pandey, B. K., & Pandey, D., 2025), are expected to deliver lots of advantages like; fewer accidents, less maintenance time, and better quality. The estab lishment cost of these AI systems is high and this has become a challenge for manufacturers thereby requiring cost- benefit analysis (CBA) consistently to evaluate the monetary and safety gains on such advancement. This work (Wang, Y., & Yang, X., 2025) seeks to adopt a research- based approach in assessing the cost- benefit analysis of AI safety enhancements in automotive production in a manner that can be easily understood and implemented by the manufacturers (Khadka, M. et al., 2025). The first step in the analysis involves data preprocessing, where encoding categorical variables is important so as to plan non- numerical data for the machine models. Some of the attributes in automotive manufacturing, including types of AI systems (Sheela, M. S. et al., 2025), safety mechanisms, and manufacturing tech niques, are categorical by nature. One- hot encoding or feature- label encoding, these categorical data are encoded into numerical forms to feed into the machine learning model analysis since all the possibilities are accounted for in safety and cost impacts (Satheesh, N. et al., 2025). © 2025 by IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.},
