	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly integrated into systems designed for older adults, offering significant improvements in health, mobility, and digital interaction. These applications, while promising, require careful examination of their ethical implications to ensure they are aligned with the rights, preferences, and lived experiences of aging populations. This examination must also confront the persistence of ageism and the underrepresentation of older adults in technology development. In this study, we analyze how these factors contribute to the general lack of acceptance of AI-related technology among older adults. Additionally, we address human factors issues specific to the geriatric community, such as accessibility, usability, and the need for personalized approaches that respect older adults’ autonomy and dignity. Through a review of current trends in agentic AI, we propose the Elder, Caregiver, Agent, and Practitioner-in-the-Loop (E-CAPIL) framework, which is a human-centered model that integrates agentic AI with elder, caregiver and practitioner feedback loops to address these ethical challenges. E-CAPIL provides a structured approach to embedding privacy, explainability, adaptive autonomy, and regulatory compliance into gerontechnology systems. We highlight the importance of interdisciplinary collaboration in geriatrics engineering to guide the responsible deployment of such frameworks. By addressing these ethical challenges, we aim to contribute to the advancement of AI technologies that support the aging population, ensuring they are both beneficial and respectful of their needs and rights. ©2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Ageism; Agentic AI; AI Ethics; Artificial Intelligence; Compliance; E-CAPIL Framework; elder care; geriatrics; Gerontechnology; Gerotechnology; Privacy},
	keywords = {Artificial intelligence; Ethical technology; Human computer interaction; Human engineering; Information systems; Regulatory compliance; Ageism; Agentic artificial intelligence; Artificial intelligence ethic; Compliance; Elder care; Elder, caregiver, agent, and practitioner-in-the-loop framework; Gerontechnology; Gerotechnology; Older adults; Privacy; Geriatrics},
