	affiliations = {Silesian University of Technology, Akademicka 16, Gliwice, 44-100, Poland},
	abstract = {- Legal document processing demands solutions that balance precision, scalability, and privacy. This study presents a locally deployed system integrating Large Language Models (LLMs) with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), operating entirely offline to ensure data confidentiality. We evaluate five text-splitting algorithms—Token, Sentence, Window, Semantic, and Hierarchical splitters—using 1,000 questions from 52 Polish legal documents. Results highlight the Window Splitter as the most stable, achieving 92% accuracy and resilience to parameter variations, while the Semantic Splitter shows a 15% precision gain in scaled tests. The system’s privacy-centric design, combined with hierarchical text structuring and prompt optimization, demonstrates applicability beyond law, including healthcare and finance. By addressing both technical efficiency and ethical data handling, this work advances the development of trustworthy AI for critical domains, with future research targeting adaptive chunking strategies. © 2025 IEEE.},
