	affiliations = {James Cook University, Australia; Curtin University, Australia; Australian New Zealand Social Work Welfare Education & Research (ANZSWWER), Australia},
	abstract = {The explosion of artificial intelligence (AI) capabilities presents new and emerging challenges for the ethical and responsible use of AI in social work and human services. The “alignment problem”, as it is known, contends that AI capabilities are scaling much faster than our ability to align them with human interests and values, which by their very nature are ill-defined. Consequently, ethical problems such as epistemic unreliability, a collapse of institutional trust, and the perpetuation of social injustices are likely to emerge without determined and creative vigilance. The nature and scope of regulatory responses needed to focus AI capabilities in a safe, ethical, and socially just manner appear to be hotly contested and poorly developed. This chapter critically examines AI ethics and regulation in social work practice, education, and research. This chapter begins with a case of unethical practice, highlighting the current and looming ethical challenges presented by AI. We appraise work being undertaken to address AI misalignment and consider ethical, educational, and institutional challenges; responses to AI; and regulatory protections for social work practice, education, research, and scholarly publishing. We conclude with principles that are important for ongoing critical interrogation of the ethical and regulatory needs for AI deployments in the human services sector. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Goetz Ottmann and Carolyn Noble; individual chapters, the contributors.},
	keywords = {Artificial intelligence; Alignment Problems; Case-studies; Ethical implications; Ethical problems; Human services; Institutional trust; Practice researches; Scalings; Social work; Work practices; Ethical technology},
