	affiliations = {Yildiz Technical University, Istanbul, Turkey},
	abstract = {As AI becomes more present in business settings, there grows discourse around the professional ethics of its application. Underlining this ethical debate is the unfortunate issue of ethics washing, which many corporations exploit to skirt scrutiny. They engage in massive theater about responsible AI conduct without doing much to implement concrete ethical safeguards. Also, other types of “machinewashing.” In this context, it would describe misleading claims advanced to hide the unethical uses of AI from stakeholders, disinformation, lavishly presented evidence, ambiguous verbal output, and confusingly selective information omission, to make it look like the supposedly quite ethical position is being maintained. How much of this is actual bias in AI systems used to manipulate outcomes further clouds the transparency of decision-making based on AI. Machinewashing has become a concern for those used to online credibility in today’s society, where trust in AI technology is at stake. This section will investigate the processes and implications connected with machinewashing by evaluating the consequences on a company’s reputation and the ethical deployment of AI technologies. The research basically might state that machinewashing may offer certain advantages, leading to social judgment rather than directly damaging a company’s reputation and eroding stakeholder trust. A sustainable approach to handling AI will see ethical guidelines become embedded in an organization and actively curb itself against the deployment of any unethical tactics in communication and utilization of AI technologies. So be aware of machinewashing. What impact can it have on our digital reputation? It’s timely to think of the ethics of intelligence while also leaning on transparency in AI practice. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2025.},
