	affiliations = {VR Siddhartha Engineering College, Dept. of CSE, Vijayawada, India},
	abstract = {The increasing deployment of machine learning models in critical sectors such as finance, healthcare, and employment has amplified concerns regarding algorithmic fairness, bias explanation, and model interpretability. Traditional fairness techniques often emphasize bias detection and mitigation but fall short in offering intuitive explanations for non-technical users. This project proposes an Automated Bias Explanation Framework designed to bridge this gap by integrating explainable AI (XAI) methodologies for bias analysis and visualization. The framework leverages SHAP (SHapley Additive Explanations) to attribute model decisions to input features and employs counterfactual explanations to explore minimal changes required to alter outcomes. Fairness metrics, including Statistical Parity Difference, Equalized Odds, and Disparate Impact, are computed to systematically quantify algorithmic fairness. The system is evaluated using the UCI Adult Income dataset, critically analyzing how sensitive attributes such as gender and race influence model predictions. The findings uncover latent biases within ML models and offer clear, interpretable insights for policy makers, business leaders, and AI auditors. Through its focus on bias explanation, model interpretability, and transparent fairness reporting, the project aims to foster greater transparency, accountability, and trust in AI-driven decision-making, making fairness assessments accessible to non-technical stakeholders. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Bias Explanation; Counterfactual Explanations; Explainable AI; SHAP; Transparency},
