	affiliations = {Nueva Ecija University of Science and Technology, Philippines},
	abstract = {This study presents the development of an AI-driven classification framework using a DenseNet convolutional neural network (CNN) to differentiate between real and AI-generated (fake) images. With the increasing sophistication of generative models, distinguishing authentic visual content from synthetic ones has become a critical challenge in digital media authentication and artificial intelligence ethics. The proposed DenseNet architecture leverages dense connectivity and feature reuse mechanisms to efficiently extract both low-level texture features and high-level semantic representations, ensuring optimal feature propagation and gradient flow. The model was trained and validated on a balanced dataset of real and AI-generated images. Results demonstrated outstanding performance, achieving an overall accuracy of 99.61%, with precision, recall, and F1-scores all exceeding 99% for both classes. The training and validation loss curves showed rapid convergence, indicating strong generalization and stable learning behavior. Furthermore, the confusion matrix revealed only two minor misclassifications out of 512 validation samples, emphasizing the model's robustness and reliability. These findings affirm the potential of DenseNet as an effective and accurate tool for AI-generated content detection. The study concludes that DenseNet's architecture provides a viable framework for digital content authenticity verification and recommends extending the dataset to include generative outputs from newer AI models to enhance resilience against evolving synthesis techniques. © 2025 IEEE.},
