	affiliations = {Sri Krishna Arts and Science College, Department of Electronics and Communication Systems, Coimbatore, 641008, India; Doodil2r, Coimbatore, 641042, India},
	abstract = {The swift proliferation of consumer electronics has complicated the management of electronic garbage, or 'e-waste,' which poses threats to human health and the environment. An advanced diagnostic system can be employed to detect PCB defects and extend the lifespan of electronic equipment to address this issue. This study presents an edgebased AI framework for automated inspection and fault identification in printed circuit boards (PCBs) utilizing TensorFlow Lite for low-latency, on-device inference on a Raspberry Pi. Real-time PCB images are examined utilizing a compact convolutional neural network (CNN) to detect component-level defects and structural anomalies. An analog-to-digital converter (ADC) operates concurrently to digitize real-time monitoring of analog data collected from various inspection nodes to assess system health and identify fault locations. Cloud synchronization via Rclone and Google Drive ensures continuous model updates, while also preserving flexible test sequences for various circuit types in a GitHub repository. Compatibility with Google Colab enables modular system enhancement and remote model training. The system's compatibility with hardware environments is confirmed through experimental evaluation, demonstrating high detection accuracy, minimal inference latency, and effective resource utilization. The proposed system addresses practical challenges such as synchronization latency, data management, and security, while maintaining an equilibrium between edge processing and cloud assistance. The system facilitates sustainable electronics practices and aids in minimizing e-waste. © 2025 IEEE.},
