	abstract = {Virtue ethics is increasingly the focus of attention in AI ethics. After a short introduction to virtue ethics as a moral theory, this chapter examines the manifold conceptions of virtue that are taken up by current virtue-ethical approaches to AI. I highlight the diversity of these conceptions and suggest that this diversity itself may pose a problem: the approaches may be too theoretically inconsistent to add up to a single and powerful virtue-ethical theory that can guide us toward a good life with AI. Subsequently it is shown how the virtues are applied to both our life with AI and AI as such, focusing primarily on one particular virtue – honesty or truthfulness. The chapter concludes with some tentative remarks on the prospects of an applied virtue ethics of AI. © 2025 by John Wiley & Sons, Inc. All rights reserved.},
