	abstract = {This chapter examines the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into journalism in Africa, with a focus on Kenya, through a political economy lens. Moving beyond narratives of innovation and efficiency, the chapter argues that AI reshapes journalism by reconfiguring control, labour, profit, and sovereignty. Using fictional case studies—SAMA, an AI-powered newsroom agent, and MARA, an AI news curation system—it illustrates how AI is restructuring editorial authority and news consumption. The analysis reveals a multi-layered power dynamic where newsroom managerialism, corporate platform dependency, and state regulation converge to limit journalistic autonomy. Furthermore, the chapter examines the emergence of “sovereign AI” initiatives as African states, including Kenya, strive for technological independence, highlighting the paradox of these efforts as they risk entrenching authoritarian control. It also examines the dual role of AI in democratic discourse, which simultaneously enables civic activism and state surveillance. Finally, the chapter calls for Afrocentric, ethical, and participatory approaches to AI governance in journalism, advocating for policies and pedagogical reforms that safeguard editorial independence and pluralism. By foregrounding African perspectives, the chapter repositions AI not merely as a technological shift but as a political-economic transformation with profound implications for the future of journalism © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Trust Matsilele and Lungile A. Tshuma; individual chapters, the contributors.},
