	abstract = {As artificial intelligence systems become increasingly integrated into critical infrastructure and decision-making processes, new security vulnerabilities have emerged that pose significant risks to both system integrity and sustainable development goals. This survey presents a comprehensive examination of AI security challenges through a structured four-tier taxonomy that categorizes threats across data, model, system, and governance levels. Through detailed analysis of existing defense mechanisms, including adversarial training, differential privacy, and secure federated learning-we identify fundamental trade-offs between security robustness, system performance, and long-term sustainability. Our investigation reveals substantial gaps in current governance frameworks and highlights the critical importance of interdisciplinary collaboration in addressing these challenges. The paper contributes: (1) a novel taxonomic framework for AI security threats, (2) comprehensive comparative analysis of defense strategies, (3) integration of security considerations with sustainability principles, and (4) identification of key research directions for trustworthy AI development. Our findings indicate that achieving both secure and sustainable AI systems requires coordinated efforts spanning technical innovation, regulatory development, and ethical implementation. © 2025 IEEE.},
