	affiliations = {Business Studies, Uppsala University, Sweden; Business Studies, Uppsala University, Sweden; Uppsala University, Sweden},
	abstract = {This chapter, which is conceptual in nature but also includes data from a large Swedish bank, investigates how organizations’ risk governance and management can relate to and translate the wave of public regulation surrounding AI technology from legal and accounting perspectives. Two distinct perspectives emerge from research on regulation, governance, and AI systems. The first perspective views AI as a new object for risk management, i.e., control of AI. In the second perspective, AI is viewed as a tool for business management, i.e., control with AI. In the space between regulatory demands and organizational ambitions, various tensions between the two perspectives are identified and discussed. This chapter finds that AI technology, both on its own and in conjunction with the recent wave of AI regulation at the EU level, has resulted in increased demands on organizational governance systems and an increased interest in leveraging AI for automated control in financial firms. The main conclusion of this chapter is that this leads to reinforcing dynamics where increased control with AI is likely to lead to an increasing need for control of AI to ensure the continued stability of the financial system. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Einar Iveroth, Jan Lindvall and Johan Magnusson; individual chapters, the contributors.},
	keywords = {Artificial intelligence; Automation; Information management; Public policy; System stability; AI systems; AI Technologies; Business management; Governance systems; Organisational; Organization risk; Perspective views; Risk governance; Risks management; Swedishs; Risk management},
