	year = {2025},
	journal = {2025 2nd International Conference on Computing and Data Science, ICCDS 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ICCDS64403.2025.11209732},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105023883657?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Sri Ramachandra Institute of Higher Education and Research, Department of Artificial Intelligence and Data Analytics, Chennai, India; Sri Ramachandra Institute of Higher Education and Research, Department of Bioinformatics, Chennai, India},
	abstract = {Lung cancer remains the leading cause of cancer-related mortality worldwide, emphasizing the necessity for early and biologically interpretable diagnostic frameworks. Traditional deep learning approaches in imaging and genomics have demonstrated promise; however, their limited interpretability and modality isolation impede clinical adoption. This study presents a multi-modal framework integrating explainable machine learning across genomic and radiological domains to enhance biomarker discovery and classification performance in lung cancer diagnosis. Gene expression data from The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) is utilized to train Random Forest classifiers for predicting key oncogenic mutation statuses, including EGFR, KRAS, and ALK, achieving an accuracy of 93.5%. SHAP-based feature attribution identifies critical genomic markers aligned with established biological pathways in tumor progression and therapy response. Concurrently, thoracic CT scans from the LIDC-IDRI dataset are processed using a convolutional neural network (CNN) augmented with a variational autoencoder (VAE) for synthetic image generation to address data imbalance. Grad-CAM and SHAP techniques validate salient radiomic features, such as nodule morphology, spiculation, and internal texture, ensuring transparent model reasoning. The fusion of genomic and imaging modalities significantly enhances classification performance, achieving an AUC of 96.3%, compared to 91.8% with imaging alone and 89.7% with gene expression alone. Gene set enrichment analysis reveals that the top predictive genes are enriched in clinically relevant pathways, including apoptosis, EGFR signaling, and cell cycle regulation. Ablation studies confirm the complementary value of each modality, underscoring the necessity of holistic approaches in clinical AI. By combining high predictive accuracy with interpretability across data types, the proposed framework advances radiogenomic lung cancer detection while setting a precedent for deploying trustworthy AI systems in precision oncology. © 2025 IEEE.},
