	abstract = {The evolution of Artificial intelligence (AI) has opened a new window for the public institutions to adopt automated work-process. Healthcare sectors have been facing consequences of digitalization due to their inclination over modern technologies for efficient handling of vast amounts of data that are being generated from healthcare systems comprising images, texts and administration reports. AI-driven solutions are beneficial for health systems in prediction of probable consequences and automation of work-process but this comes along with challenges in patient data security and trustworthiness of the automation models. The study provides an evaluation on the implementation of statistical analysis to determine how data governance practices are carried out in healthcare AI. The dataset used for performing the statistical analysis has been developed by collecting information across hospitals in the UK signifying realistic patient data. However, the name of the hospitals has been kept anonymous due to ethical and privacy concerns. Due to the fact that AI has occupied a prioritised position in the field of healthcare, it becomes necessary to develop concern on transparency and trust. The statistical analysis has been carried out using MS Excel to undergo analytics of datasets collected from Electronic Health Records (EHRs) and clinical data repositories . These analytical processes include the use of descriptive statistics to gain insights into samples and their impact on the data. Furthermore, t-tests have been carried out using ANOVA and Pivot tables and carts such that visualisation of the data can be done in terms of their quality testing. Findings from the study indicates that the use of data governance helps in protecting privacy and confidentiality of patient records stored across institutions offering services through AI. © 2025 IEEE.},
