	abstract = {This concluding chapter explores how educators can shape the future of AI in higher education through reflective, participatory, and purposeful practice. Drawing from narrative case studies, Rebecca Allen and Allison Lester illustrate how faculty and students are co-creating AI policies, tools, and training ecosystems that center agency, ethics, and inclusion. The chapter revisits major themes from the book—AI ethics, faculty identity, student agency, and participatory design—while highlighting practical strategies such as prompt engineering, interdisciplinary collaboration, and localized AI training. The authors argue for shifting from solely “how-to” guidance to “why-to” engagement, emphasizing that faculty must feel supported and valued—not displaced—amid technological change. With recommendations for institutional policy, assessment redesign, and cross-curricular integration, this chapter offers a hopeful yet grounded roadmap for navigating generative AI in higher education. Ultimately, the authors affirm that literacy, not blind adoption, is the goal—and that human-centered, student-inclusive approaches can guide meaningful AI integration, no matter how quickly the tools evolve. © 2025 The Editor(s) (if applicable) and The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG.},
