	affiliations = {Computer Science and Engineering, Vishnu Institute of Technology, Bhimavaram, India},
	abstract = {Beaches attract millions of tourists annually, offering recreational and economic value but posing risks such as rip currents, coastal erosion, and extreme weather conditions. Existing safety tools provide only static forecasts and general hazard warnings, lacking real-time, locationspecific data and predictive analytics. We propose an AIpowered coastal tourism application that integrates real-time environmental data from APIs including StormGlass, InCOIS, OpenWeather, and Foursquare to predict hazardous conditions. The platform provides GPS-based beach searches, dynamic hazard alerts, route optimization, and personalized safety recommendations, supporting disaster preparedness through early warnings and efficient resource guidance. SeaAegis features a multilingual interface and employs ensemble and reinforcement learning algorithms, adapting to environmental changes and user input to enhance predictions and routing. Evaluation results on test and simulated data indicate improvement over existing platforms, including hazard prediction accuracy of 92% (baseline: 75%), updates in 2 seconds (baseline: 10 seconds), coverage of 7 environmental parameters (baseline: 3), and a user satisfaction score of 4.7/5 (baseline: 3.8/5). Future work includes integrating satellite imagery and on-site IoT sensors, improving machine learning models, providing personalized recommendations, expanding coverage to more beaches with crowd density predictions, mobile notifications for hazards and optimal visit timings. © 2025 IEEE.},
