	abstract = {Digital twin (DT) ecosystems are emerging as a cornerstone of Industry 4.0, healthcare, and vehicular systems, with 6G networks providing the high-bandwidth, low-latency foundation for real-time operation. Yet, federated learning (FL) across distributed DT nodes raises significant privacy, security, and interoperability concerns, particularly in the absence of unified standards. This article proposes a standards-aligned, privacy-preserving FL framework for 6G-enabled DTs that integrates differential privacy, homomorphic encryption, and secure multi-party computation within a blockchain-coordinated architecture. We present a layered design for DT clients, edge/base-station nodes, and secure aggregators, ensuring compliance with ISO/ IEC 27701 and IEEE P7002 governance guidelines. A comprehensive threat model is detailed, covering poisoning, backdoor, Sybil, and inference attacks, along with countermeasures that enhance trust, accountability, and resilience. Experimental results show <4% accuracy loss while achieving ε ≈ 0.7 privacy and up to 25% improved attack resilience, meeting near real-time latency constraints. Finally, we map findings to IEEE, ISO/IEC, and 3GPP initiatives, offering a roadmap for interoperable, auditable, and secure DT deployments in next-generation communication infrastructures. © 2017 IEEE.},
	keywords = {Blockchain; Differential privacy; Industry 4.0; Interoperability; Network security; Privacy-preserving techniques; Regulatory compliance; Differential privacies; Healthcare systems; High bandwidth; Homomorphic-encryptions; ISO/IEC; Learning frameworks; Low latency; Privacy preserving; Real-time operation; Vehicular systems; ISO Standards},
