	abstract = {The existing emotion and sentiment analysis systems mostly rely on a single input channel, which are text, speech, or vision. These unimodal systems cause frequent errors, incomplete, unclear deductions in natural, noisy, or unclear human communication situations, resulting in margins of error. This paper introduces a multimodal AI framework named Sentimind. The Sentimind integrates text-based, acoustic/voice-based, and visual/facial cues into a unified early-fusion pipeline. It is designed and optimised for near real time deductions. Also, it combines a fine-tuned text transformer encoder, a speech encoder that is based on MFCC and prosodic features, along with a self-developed CNN for facial-contour and expression extraction. The presentation of the system architecture, detailed methodology, literature-derived benchmarks/results, latency, and deployment. Simulation results indicate the improved classification, accuracy, and acceptable latency, enabling to have applications in healthcare, education, driving safety, and human computer interaction. The Sentimind is built on the motivation to overcome the limits of the unimodal emotion systems that miss accuracy and misclassify, reducing the efficiency. The motivation behind Sentimind is that it provides a solution by the proposed framework that works on the fusion of all three layers, text, speech and vision to provide the best and accurate results.  © 2025 IEEE.},
