	abstract = {This study investigates the ethical implications and cultural considerations in implementing AI technologies within English as a Foreign Language (EFL) education in Saudi Arabia. The research employed a mixed-methods approach, collecting data from 27 EFL professors across various Saudi higher education institutions. Through questionnaires and structured surveys, the study examined educator perspectives on AI implementation, ethical concerns, cultural preservation, and professional development needs. Key findings reveal significant tensions between technological advancement and cultural preservation, with 66.7% of educators expressing concerns about AI's impact on traditional teaching values. The study identified critical gaps in ethical frameworks, particularly regarding plagiarism detection (68% reporting increased risks) and data privacy concerns. Cultural integration emerged as a paramount consideration, with notable disparities between urban and rural institutions in AI adoption rates and infrastructure support. Professional development needs were highlighted, with 84% of educators prioritizing technical training while struggling to balance AI implementation with cultural sensitivity. The research concludes that successful AI integration in Saudi EFL education requires a culturally-contained approach that aligns with traditional principles and preserves Arabic linguistic traditions. Recommendations include developing comprehensive AI ethics guidelines, implementing culturally-sensitive training programs, and establishing robust assessment frameworks. These findings contribute to the growing body of knowledge on culturally appropriate AI integration in educational contexts and provide practical guidelines for institutions navigating the complex intersection of technology, culture, and pedagogy in Saudi Arabia. © 2025 by the authors.},
