	abstract = {Generative artificial intelligence (GAI), conceptualised as a technology system based on algorithms and models designed to create text, audio, images, videos, and code, is assuming a prominent role in news production globally. GAI is not only reconfiguring how news is reported, delivered and consumed but also has the capacity to optimise and enhance efficiencies in news production in a media landscape where cost-cutting has become ubiquitous. Despite the affordances promised by GAI, its adoption and use in news production have raised several ethical questions and concerns that challenge fundamental principles of journalism. Considering this background, this scoping review of existing literature on GAI and journalism practice premises itself on a conceptual framework consisting of traditional journalism ethics to examine and synthesise existing literature on the ethical issues and concerns arising because of the adoption and use of GAI in news production processes. The study further examines the different mechanisms that journalists and news organisations are using to mitigate the risks posed by GAI. The findings of the study show that the adoption of GAI in news production processes raises ethical issues and concerns that relate to transparency, bias, accuracy, reliability, fabrication of information, misinformation, user privacy and rights, sensitivity and empathy, and job displacement. However, to mitigate these concerns and risks, journalists, news organisations, and national governments have adopted multi-pronged approaches that include human supervision, AI guidelines and code of ethics, AI literacy for journalists and audiences, AI regulation, and collaboration between journalists and programmers in AI and GAI design. The findings have several implications for journalists, news organisations, national governments, and policymakers in developing countries where GAI uptake has been slow and there are currently no mechanisms to guide journalists and 