	affiliations = {Dayananda Sagar University, Dept. of Cse (Data Science), Bangalore, India},
	abstract = {In an increasingly digital world, understanding emotions expressed through textual content has become vital across sectors such as healthcare, education, and digital mental wellness. This paper presents a multilingual, realtime emotion detection system that combines Natural Language Processing (NLP) and Machine Learning (ML) to identify emotional tone in user-submitted text. The system incorporates language de- tection using langdetect, followed by translation via APIs like googletrans, enabling it to handle diverse linguistic inputs. After preprocessing, emotional classification is performed using models such as LSTM and fine-tuned BERT, trained on curated datasets like GoEmotions. The detected emotion is then visualized using emotion-specific imagery to enhance user interaction and awareness. To address long-term emotional trends, the system tracks user emotion history within a session and triggers mental health recommendations when recurring negative emotions are observed. Hosted on a modular Flask backend, the system ensures data privacy and is easily extensible for future enhancements such as chatbot integration, speech-based input, or emotion timeline analytics. This emotion detection architecture echoes the evolving paradigm in healthcare predictive systems, where the integration of clinical knowledge with data-driven models has proven effective. Just as hybrid AI frameworks in heart failure prediction combine rule-based reasoning and ML for early intervention, our system blends contextual understanding with predictive modeling to offer a soft-intervention approach for mental wellness. By combining explainability, adaptability, and multilingual support, this system contributes meaningfully to the field of affective computing and responsible AI in real- world emotional support applications. Trained on the GoEmotions dataset, our system achieves an average F1-score of 0.85 across six emotion categories. It supports multilingual emotion detection with session-based monitoring, offering mental health suggestions like helpline links when negative emotions persist. © 2025 IEEE.},
