	abstract = {The increasing contribution of corporate carbon emissions to climate change and public health degradation necessitates a robust, interdisciplinary framework for legal accountability and impact assessment. The study introduces an AI-augmented legal analytics system designed to evaluate the public health consequences of corporate carbon emissions by integrating environmental, legal, and epidemiological datasets. The proposed model employs spatio-temporal alignment of satellite-based pollutant concentration data (Sentinel-5P, GHGsat) with public health metrics (hospitalization, mortality, morbidity), and incorporates legal compliance data extracted from databases like LexisNexis and Westlaw. Natural language processing techniques - such as Named Entity Recognition and transformer-based models (Legal-BERT, CaseLaw-BART) - enable extraction of obligations, violations, and litigation precedents. Graph Neural Networks and Double Machine Learning models are utilized to quantify the causal relationship between emissions and health outcomes, while a cross-domain knowledge graph links corporate entities, pollutants, legal norms, and medical conditions. Results show that the LSTM-Attention network achieved a predictive accuracy of 91.6% in estimating respiratory hospital admissions, while Bayesian Neural Networks reached 88.5% accuracy with uncertainty estimation. The Legal Environmental Non-Compliance Index (LENCI) accurately ranked corporations based on violation history and associated health impact. Knowledge graph inferences exposed latent legal-pollution-health associations, enhancing regulatory visibility. The AI-augmented pipeline offers a comprehensive, interpretable framework that facilitates policy-making, regulatory enforcement, and early health risk detection. By combining legal reasoning with machine learning and geospatial analytics, the study demonstrates a new frontier in environmental governance and public health protection. © 2025 IEEE.},
