	affiliations = {King Mongkut's University of Technology North Bangkok, Department of Network and Information Security Management, Bangkok, Thailand},
	abstract = {Cyber threats continue to grow in scale and complexity, making reactive defenses increasingly ineffective. Artificial intelligence (AI) provides strong detection capabilities, but the lack of transparency in black-box models reduces trust and slows adoption in critical environments. This paper introduces the Explainable Cyber Crime Triangle Framework (XAI-CCTF), an extension of our earlier work that integrates Attention, SHAP, and LIME into the offender, target, and guardian modules. The aim is to combine high predictive performance with explanations that analysts can rely on. Tests on five different datasets show that XAI-CCTF maintains high detection accuracy (93.8%) with only a slight increase in false positives (2.4%). We also introduce two quantitative metrics-Explanation Consistency (0.847) and Feature Importance Stability (0.791)-to verify explanation quality. Additional experiments on adversarial robustness and a proxy human-centered validation confirm that the system provides useful, reliable outputs in Security Operations Center (SOC) conditions. These results show that explainability can be built into the framework itself without giving up performance, supporting trustworthy and regulation-aligned use of AI in proactive cybersecurity.  © 2025 IEEE.},
