	abstract = {Artificial Intelligence (AI) has emerged as a powerful tool for automatic music generation. However, existing approaches often face challenges in balancing musical coherence, creativity, and diversity. Moreover, many models neglect critical legal and ethical considerations, such as copyright infringement, ambiguous authorship, and cultural misrepresentation. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a multistage pipeline for symbolic music generation that integrates advanced deep learning architectures, including Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), Long Short-Term Memory networks (LSTMs), Transformers, Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), and Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs). These models are trained on a broad range of symbolic music datasets encompassing Bach chorales, folk melodies, expressive piano pieces, and emotion-labeled compositions. Empirical evaluation demonstrates that Transformer-based models are particularly effective in preserving long-term musical structure and introducing stylistic variation, while VAEs and GANs contribute to the generation of innovative and non-traditional musical motifs. In addition, the work incorporates a review of international AI ethics guidelines and underscores the need for transparency and collaboration with human artists. The proposed pipeline not only advances the state of symbolic music generation but also contributes to responsible and culturally aware AI-driven creativity. © 2025 IEEE.},
