	title = {Self-Adaptive AI Framework for QoS Optimisation and Energy Efficiency in Wireless Sensor Networks},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {2025 3rd World Conference on Communication and Computing, WCONF 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/WCONF64849.2025.11233718},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105029711945?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Chitkara University Institute of Engineering and Technology, Chitkara University, Punjab, India},
	abstract = {In this paper, we design a self-adapting AI framework that can optimise the Quality of Service (QoS) and energy savings of wireless sensor networks (WSNs) and intelligent spaces. The proposed framework uses reinforcement learning (RL) to dynamically tune important network parameters (node duty cycles, transmission power and data sampling rates), in response to real-time environmental feedback. The framework is designed to solve a problem of striking the right balance between energy efficiency and sustaining good QoS in a resource-constrained and adaptive environment. The simulation results indicate the rivalry of the proposed framework in comparison with a baseline fixed-policy system in vital aspects. The overall latency is minimised by 31.5%, from 130 ms to 89 ms, with a focus on the increase in efficiency of communication. Relative to Packet Delivery Ratio (PDR), there is an increment of 12 %, which was initially at 86 percent and changed to 96.3 %. The system further realises the reduction in energy per bit of 36.8 %, which is from 12.5 5J/bit to 7.9 J/bit, improving energy efficiency significantly. Moreover, the lifetime of nodes is increased by 37.1 %, i.e. 22.4 hours to 30.7 hours, which shows that the framework is efficient enough to not only save its power but also maintain its efficiency. These findings ensure that the self-adaptive AI framework is highly usable in QoS- and energy-constrained IoT with a lot of scale. The framework is resilient with long-term sustainability because it constantly learns the conditions of networks and adjusts its behavior hence delivering promising self-sustaining solutions to smart environments in the future.  © 2025 IEEE.},
