	abstract = {The ethics of Artificial Intelligence and intelligent technologies has become an increasing trend nowadays because of the necessity to create AI systems that value human aspects, usability, and well-being. The paper is an in-depth examination of developing and testing of human-centred AI (HCAI) methodologies. This introduce new theoretical frameworks according to which the process of human-AI interaction is reinvented as the co-adaptive, clear, and morally justified evolution. Special focus is made on participatory design approaches which include the users in the lifecycle of AI development - conceptualization to deployment. Research proposes the frameworks and tools that would suit the context of aligning the AI functionality to human cognition, emotions, and contextual requirements with the examples of the concrete undertaking approaches. Further the paper accesses the prototyping methods of iterative testing, introduce new metrics and tools to evaluate user-experience, trust, and fairness and to gauge the psychological influence. By using cross-cultural case studies, paper point out the learning experiences in how to design inclusive artificial intelligence solutions to various demographic and socio-technical environments. The longitudinal testing also helps identify trends in adaptation and satisfaction of users as time passes and provides cues to take action in refining AI systems in an endlessly iterative process. This paper contributes a unified perspective on HCAI design, advocating for interdisciplinary, ethics-informed, and user-empowered methodologies. © 2025 IEEE.},
