	abstract = {Digital transformation in Small Island Developing States (SIDS) occurs within a challenging context of limited resources, geographic dispersion, climate vulnerability, and competing national priorities. Major development institutions offer comprehensive frameworks; however, their pillar-based and maturity assessment approaches inadequately address the persistent paradoxes that define SIDS policymaking. We present the Paradox-Pathway Framework (PPF), a strategic visioning methodology for ten-to-twenty year horizons that treats contradictions as strategic opportunities rather than problems to eliminate. Application to the Maldives demonstrates a three-phase approach: Diagnosis, Strategy Formulation, and Vision Articulation. Where conventional frameworks assume sufficient resources to address all priorities simultaneously, PPF's dialectical logic converts inherent tensions into sources of strategic differentiation and national resilience. Stakeholder validation (n=45) across government, academia, civil society, state-owned enterprises, and the private sector showed strong framework resonance (100% agreement, 62.5% strongly agree), ambitious but achievable pathways (87.5%), and unanimous preferences over traditional gap analysis (62.5% rating "more useful,"37.5% "much more useful"). These findings indicate that paradox-centered approaches may better serve SIDS strategic planning than linear diagnostic models. © 2025 IEEE.},
