	affiliations = {King Mongkut's University of Technology North Bangkok (KMUTNB), Division of Information and Communication Technology for Education, Thailand},
	abstract = {This research aims to develop an integrated conceptual framework for enhancing engineering education through artificial intelligence (AI) support via the Communities of Practice (CoP) mechanism. The study proposes a two-phase research methodology in which both phases complement each other. Phase 1 employs bibliographic analysis using VOSviewer to map keyword co-occurrence networks. The mapping results reveal three main clusters: (1) AI teaching and learning practice clusters (e.g., chatbots and generative AI); (2) learning approaches, including active learning, cooperative learning, and individual learning; and (3) cross-dimensional issues related to information security and AI ethics, which are essential for ensuring credibility in educational contexts. Phase 2 synthesizes relevant literature to define the components of three main dimensions: AI-enhanced learning, engineering communities of practice, and engineering education. The proposed framework integrates bibliographic analysis and literature synthesis and highlights how AI-powered personalization, intelligent tutoring systems, and adaptive analytics can be leveraged to create more sustainable learning experiences. CoP facilitates knowledge exchange and mutual learning among learners, while the CDIO (Conceive-Design-Implement-Operate) approach strengthens problem-solving skills and engineering innovation. In addition, the integration of emerging technologies supports active learning, real-time assessment, and scalable infrastructure for engineering education. The findings indicate that the implementation of the Engineering CoP framework can enhance learner engagement and improve the efficiency of knowledge sharing.  © 2025 IEEE.},
