	abstract = {Outer space and cyberspace are two interwoven spaces, highly dependent on each other and presenting similar legislative challenges. Interdependence between these two spaces' infrastructure can give rise to new categories of cyber-attacks enabling unauthorized control of space objects to cause further damage. It thus becomes crucial for relevant laws to account for cyber risks to space assets. This raises the question: how does the space liability framework apply to cybersecurity, in light of emerging challenges raised by technological trends of Artificial Intelligence (AI) deployment and industry trends of commercialization? The study tackles this question through doctrinal research, analyzing various components of the legal framework on space liability, cybersecurity, and AI. Given the jurisdictional peculiarities of outer space and cyberspace, the research covers the interaction of different regulatory levels: it analyzes international space law, accounts for national frameworks and industry guidelines, and draws insights from the European Union's approach to AI governance. Legal analysis is paired with a use-case tackling AI's impact on the space/cyber/liability mix. It has also been complemented with an on-field research program in Space, Cyber and Telecommunications Law hosted by the European Space Agency/European Centre for Space Law, providing insights on scholarly, industry, and institutional debates. The analysis uncovers four core findings regarding the intersection of outer space and cyberspace. First, it unveils the legal framework's fragmentation in addressing cyber threats to space systems. Second, it highlights the inadequacy of space-liability notions in covering cybersecurity. Third, it reveals insufficient enforcement mechanisms against private actors, and cybersecurity gaps in States' licensing and insurance practices. Fourth, assessing the space liability framework highlights shortfalls in applying notions of damage, causal link, and duty of care to cybersecurity cases involving AI. Based on these findings, the research concludes that the space legal framework falls short of properly covering cases involving cybersecurity, and calls for revisions to achieve a balance between leveraging technical advancements and mitigating cyber risks. Liability notions must be adapted to emerging cyber threats, types of damage, and AI characteristics. Liability allocation must consider the different actors involved in damage caused by space-objects targeted by cyber-attacks. National authorization regimes should set clear obligations and insurance requirements for private space operators to enhance their risk-management measures, also covering cyber and AI risks. © 2025 International Astronautical Federation, IAF. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Artificial Intelligence; Cybersecurity; Liability; Space Law},
	keywords = {Computer crime; Crime; Cybersecurity; International law; Network security; Space applications; Space flight; Telecommunication industry; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Cyber-attacks; Cyberspaces; Legal frameworks; Legislative challenges; Liability; Outer space; Space laws; Space objects; Artificial intelligence; Product liability},
	publisher = {International Astronautical Federation, IAF},
