	abstract = {Developing ethical artificial intelligence has become crucial, especially as advancements in machine learning lead to the deployment of AI across society. Drawing on religious and cultural values exposes lacunae in current approaches to robotics and AI, and creates opportunities to design AI for human flourishing. The Korean value of jeong (empathy, solidarity, and mutual obligation) offers an example of such cultural theology for AI development. Prioritizing jeong in AI technologies could improve human-human and, theoretically, human-robot interactions. The conjunction of theological, religious studies, and social AI approaches shows that ethical AI requires more than western ethics. If AI incorporates human-human and human-AI jeong, the challenges of surveillance, algorithmic bias, and even hypothetical superintelligence become more manageable. It is the ability of religious studies to reveal and interrogate the contributions of global cultures that makes scholars vital in twenty-first century education, policymaking, and the technological marketplace. © 2025 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
