	abstract = {Artificial intelligence is revolutionizing the delivery of individualized healthcare by offering workable answers to long-standing issues that people with disabilities face. But there is a lack of attention to crucial issues like data privacy, system adaptability, and real-time personalized intervention. In order to overcome these limitations and create a smart and safe framework tailored for the disabled community, this review explores how three AI paradigms-Generative AI, Reinforcement Learning, and Federated Learning-can be integrated. Between 2018 and 2024, we conduct a thematic analysis of over 50 peer-reviewed studies to look at how each technology, working together, supports these contributions. Generative AI, by means of technologies such as Large Language Models and Generative Adversarial Networks, facilitates the generation of varied health data and inclusive interfaces for sensory or cognitive disabilities. Our most important contribution is a new layer-based framework using FL for secure data processing, RL for ongoing adaptation, and Generative AI for multimodal interfaces. We also give usage examples in mobility, sensory, and cognitive disability areas. This review seeks to direct researchers and practitioners towards inclusive, secure, and adaptive AI-facilitated healthcare systems for disabled populations. © 2025 IEEE.},
