	abstract = {Prior research shows that users make technology decisions based on features like ease of use and usefulness, but ethical considerations and accuracy are increasingly important, especially with evolving generative AI (GenAI) tools like ChatGPT. This study develops a model for acceptable information systems (IS) use for GenAI through a scenario-based approach. Findings indicate that ethical considerations and perceived risks can overshadow traditional predictors of technology acceptance. Acceptable IS use is particularly relevant for technologies with interpretive flexibility. Personal traits, such as technological innovativeness and a moral work approach, predict perceived ease of use. While perceived ease of use and usefulness do not predict acceptable IS use in originality-focused scenarios, ease of use is significant in high-risk situations, and usefulness matters more in low-risk contexts. These findings highlight the complexity of determining and managing the acceptable use of AI and provide insights for research and practice. © 2025 International Association for Computer Information Systems.},
