	abstract = {The Internet of Things (IoT) generates vast volumes of diverse and heterogeneous data; however, ensuring its quality, integration, and interoperability remains a persistent challenge, particularly in complex IoT environments and critical infrastructure domains. Although several approaches have been proposed to address specific aspects of data quality, such as error correction and normalization, a comprehensive and standardized methodology remains underdeveloped. This paper underscores the need for a structured approach that systematically addresses issues such as erroneous data, inconsistencies, and format variability. A key gap in the literature is the absence of integrated frameworks that combine metadata enrichment, preprocessing, and data modeling to improve the usability and reliability of IoT data. Metadata plays a central role in providing semantic context, supporting traceability, and maintaining data integrity. Data modeling further contributes by enabling the organization and standardization of data, ensuring its scalability for large-scale analytical applications.This paper presents a practical framework that integrates data preprocessing, uniformization, metadata enrichment, and structured modeling to improve the usability and reliability of IoT data. A use case involving hydrogen fuel stations illustrates the applicability of the approach and its potential to support more robust and interpretable analytical processes. © 2025 IEEE.},
