	abstract = {This study explored how college students make moral decisions regarding AI-assisted academic cheating. Using a qualitative approach, we gathered responses from Technical Communication undergraduates through an open-ended online survey. Results revealed four major themes: environmental conscience, authenticity, guilt and shame, and institutional pressure. These themes highlight both individual and intersubjective dimensions of ethical reasoning. Notably, students exhibited awareness of AI's environmental footprint, suggesting a broader ethical engagement with AI beyond its use for academic dishonesty. Feelings of inauthenticity and guilt often deterred students from cheating, showing how deeply personal emotions intersect with moral decision-making. Moreover, college students described peer and faculty influence as critical, both positively and negatively, shaping their behavior. Findings underscore the teacher's pivotal role in cultivating ethical AI use, not only by modeling responsible practices but also by providing philosophical and values-based education. We recommend long-term, holistic ethical instruction integrated into curricula to equip students with the moral armamentarium to navigate AI use responsibly.  © 2025 IEEE.},
