	abstract = {The rapid advancement and widespread adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) technologies have brought forth significant challenges in understanding and managing their decision-making processes. Many AI algorithms operate as “black boxes,” making it difficult for humans to interpret their inner workings. This opacity can lead to unintended consequences, including the perpetuation of biases present in training data, resulting in discriminatory outcomes. To address these concerns, it is crucial to develop strategies for detecting and mitigating bias, ensuring fairness and equal treatment across diverse groups. Organizations must implement comprehensive AI risk management frameworks and corporate policies to effectively navigate the ethical, social, and cybersecurity challenges posed by AI technologies. These frameworks should be applicable across various industry use cases and environments, including on-premise and software-as-a-service solutions. However, before implementing risk management strategies, it is essential to first identify and understand the specific risks that need to be controlled in AI systems. © 2025 by John Wiley & Sons, Inc. All rights reserved.},
