	affiliations = {ChatVE and Pan Atlantic University, Lagos State, Nigeria; Federal Polytechnic, Ayede, Oyo State, Nigeria; Redeemers University, Osun State, Ede, Nigeria},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) is changing the information age. AI is being used to source information, produce news articles, and identify trends. Bloomberg was an early adopter of AI for Journalism using Cyborg, a program dissecting financial reports and instantly writing news stories with all relevant facts and figures. In today's world, more than traditional news platforms like newspapers, radio, televisions, people are depending on social media platforms to get the latest trending information from health, entertainment, sport to politics and lifestyle. These platforms at the same time also allow the spread of unverified information to a large section of the audience. The need to address the exposure of vulnerable children and youth to online toxic content and misinformation is critical in West Africa. As of January 2024, over 120 million Nigerians are online, according to the Nigeria Communications Commission (NCC). In tandem with the way the digital space gains prominence and raises hopes for robust digital governance, online incitement to violent extremism polarizing the society and the disruption of democratic process is also on the rise necessitating a comprehensive and multi-faceted approach to tackle this challenge. By exploring the transformative power of AI for social good, focusing on balancing innovation with responsibility and combatting information pollution to promote information integrity, we examine cross-sectoral applications of AI, focusing initiatives like Chat VE and CitiBot, which contribute to societal goals such as promoting national values and civic engagement, improving governance systems and digital information ecosystem, and enhancing information integrity. Our paper analyses frameworks including Microsoft's Responsible AI principles for ensuring fairness, transparency, and accountability in AI design and use, ethical AI deployment, and robust regulatory frameworks to mitigate potential risks. In conclusion, we argued that while data-intensive and AI-based technologies have shown potential to solve some of the world's biggest challenges, they also pose significant risks to individuals and groups. We point out critical issues that must be addressed to ensure AI's benefits are inclusive and widely accessible, the global implications of AI, particularly flawed data, algorithmic bias, the need for widespread AI literacy, and strategies for bridging the digital divide to ensure inclusive AI's benefits. As industries deploy new technologies, they must consider the ethical ramifications of their use. The responsible design and use of AI and data are difficult, but there is an urgent need for it in our current ecosystem. When organizations adopt new technologies without regard to the wider social, economic, cultural, and political contexts, they can endanger privacy and security and exacerbate inequities in ways that are difficult to reverse. © 2025 IEEE.},
