	affiliations = {Kyoto University, Graduate School of Informatics, Kyoto, Japan},
	abstract = {This paper numerically simulates how a 'moral intensity' parameter (λ), integrated into the Dil-lenberger-Sadowski dual-utility framework, transforms the Prisoner's Dilemma. The analysis reveals three critical thresholds as moral intensity increases. First, at a low level (λ ≈ 0.06), mutual cooperation emerges as a possible Nash equilibrium. Next, at a moderate level (λ ≈ 0.15), cooperation becomes the robust, risk-dominant strategy. Finally, at λ ≈ 0.16, agents begin aspiring to games with higher social outcomes. This phased 'Possibility → Robustness → Aspiration' process elucidates how morality not only resolves the dilemma but also guides agents toward higher-order cooperation, with significant implications for AI ethics and institutional design.  © 2025 IEEE.},
