	affiliations = {Chhatrapati Shahu Ji Maharaj University, India; CSJM University, Kanpur, India},
	abstract = {As artificial intelligence becomes increasingly integrated into virtual environments— shaping avatars, non- player characters (NPCs), and decision- making systems—the ethical landscape of digital worlds is being profoundly reshaped. This paper exploresthe ethical implications of AI- driven agents within virtual spaces, focusing on issues of autonomy, accountability, and moral influence. By examining how intelligent avatars and NPCs can reinforce or challenge user behaviors, perpetuate biases, or simulate moral reasoning, we argue that developers and stakeholders must adopt ethical frameworks for the design and governance of virtual AI. We analyze case studies from gaming, metaverse platforms, and educational simulations to highlight ethical tensions arising from deception, emotional manipulation, surveillance, and consent. Finally, we propose guidelines for responsible AI implementation in virtual worlds that respect user dignity, promote fairness, and encourage transparent interaction between human and synthetic agents. © 2026 by IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.},
