	affiliations = {University of Biskra, Dept. Computer Science, Biskra, Algeria},
	abstract = {The Fast Gradient Sign Method (FGSM) has contributed significantly to adversarial machine learning research since its introduction in 2014, developing from a single-step attack into a collection of methods that examine deep neural network robustness across various domains. This review examines the mathematical foundations, algorithmic innovations, and practical applications of FGSM variants, analyzing how momentum-based enhancements, input transformations, and theoretical insights have improved attack transferability from 25% to over 90% in black-box scenarios. We examine the development from basic FGSM to advanced variants including MI-FGSM, DI2-FGSM, TI-FGSM, and LinBP-based methods, while analyzing multilayered defense mechanisms that combine adversarial training, preprocessing transformations, and architectural modifications. Our analysis examines vulnerabilities in safety-critical applications, where medical imaging systems show accuracy degradation from 96% to 32% under attack, and autonomous vehicle systems face comparable challenges. The paper provides a systematic categorization of FGSM variants, theoretical convergence analysis, and evaluation of defensive strategies, concluding with emerging research directions for responsible AI deployment in adversarial environments. © 2025 IEEE.},
