	affiliations = {School of Management and Entrepreneurship, Indian Institute of Technology, India},
	abstract = {The global semiconductor chip shortage, driven by the COVID-19 pandemic, geopolitical tensions, and surging demand, has disrupted industries reliant on chips, including consumer electronics, automotive, and AI. This crisis has exposed vulnerabilities in the semiconductor supply chain, causing manufacturing slowdowns, price surges, and product delays. AI chips, essential for machine learning and deep learning, have been particularly affected, straining data centers, autonomous vehicles, and edge computing. Limited advanced fabrication facilities and reliance on key manufacturers like TSMC, Intel, and Samsung have exacerbated the issue. Governments and tech firms are now investing in domestic semiconductor production and R&D to mitigate future risks. This chapter explores the causes, impacts, and strategic responses to the shortage, emphasizing supply chain diversification, technological advancements, and policy initiatives to strengthen global semiconductor resilience. The crisis underscores the need for self-sufficiency and innovations in AI chip architecture. © 2025 by IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved.},
