	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) is transforming water management by enhancing real-time monitoring, optimizing resource allocation, and strengthening disaster preparedness. AI-powered smart water systems enable continuous quality assessment, efficient agricultural irrigation, and predictive flood management, fostering sustainability and resilience. By integrating machine learning, big data analytics, and Internet of Things (IoT) technologies, AI enhances water conservation, minimizes wastage, and improves energy efficiency. These innovations contribute to sustainable water governance by enabling data-driven decision-making and predictive infrastructure maintenance. However, AI integration in water management presents significant environmental concerns. AI-driven applications require substantial computational power, leading to high energy consumption and increased carbon footprints from data centers. The training of deep learning models demands extensive electricity, often sourced from fossil fuels, exacerbating greenhouse gas emissions. Additionally, AI systems rely on massive data processing and storage, intensifying energy demands and raising concerns about electronic waste and resource depletion. The automation of water distribution and disaster response, while improving efficiency, also introduces ethical challenges related to accountability, transparency, and data security. Further challenges include data quality, accessibility, and interoperability. Incomplete or biased datasets can compromise AI model predictions, leading to flawed decision-making in water resource management. High costs associated with AI infrastructure—such as sensors, computational hardware, and software—also hinder widespread adoption, particularly in resource-limited regions. A balanced approach is needed to harness AI’s benefits while mitigating environmental drawbacks. Despite these challenges, AI-driven solutions have shown significant promise in enhancing water sustainability. Case studies highlight AI’s success in optimizing irrigation systems, predicting droughts and floods, and improving water treatment processes. Moving forward, adopting energy-efficient algorithms, utilizing renewable-powered data centers, and implementing ethical AI frameworks are essential to reducing AI’s environmental footprint. A comprehensive understanding of AI’s benefits and trade-offs is crucial for its responsible integration into global water management. By embracing a sustainable approach, AI can support resilient and adaptive water management systems that address current and future challenges. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Smriti Agarwal and Manoj Chandra Garg; individual chapters, the contributors. All rights reserved.},
	keywords = {Behavioral research; Big data; Carbon footprint; Decision making; Disaster prevention; Disasters; Electronic Waste; Environmental management; Ethical aspects; Flood control; Floods; Green computing; Greenhouse gases; Irrigation; Sustainable development; Wastes; Water conservation; Water management; Agricultural irrigation; Continuous quality assessments; Datacenter; Decisions makings; Disaster preparedness; Flood management; Real time monitoring; Resources allocation; Water system; Waters managements; Economic and social effects},
