	abstract = {The EU's AI legislation has initiated the world's first concrete regulation of AI. With the adoption of uniform rules, the legislation seeks to position Europe as a reliable global center for AI. These rules guide AI development, marketing, and utilization within the EU. The AI Act's implementation ensures the safety of AI systems and the adherence to fundamental rights and values. The EU is committed to fostering the Single Market in AI development by encouraging investment and innovation and enhancing governance and oversight. AI is greatly utilized in cyber security. For example, using AI, anomaly-based intrusion detection systems (IDSs) evaluate an entity's typical behaviour. Any significant deviation from this usual behaviour is thus seen by the system as abnormal and hence as an intrusion. According to Sommer and Paxson [1], AI enables the identification of entirely new threats as well as updates to established attacks. IDSs may be further divided into groups based on the kinds of algorithms they use. To create intelligent solutions, AI improves the ability to extract knowledge from massive amounts of data. With little assistance from humans, an AI system may provide data-driven conclusions, forecasts, and assessments based on what it has discovered from prior data. Since AI is widely used in cyber security and the use of AI is regulated by the new AI Act, the effects of this law must also be addressed in cyber resilience education. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2025.},
