	affiliations = {Johns Hopkins University, Whiting School of Engineering, APL, Laurel, MD, United States},
	abstract = {The increasing deployment of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in resource-constrained edge environments necessitates a robust framework for ensuring trustworthiness. This paper introduces the Trust Degradation Mode Effect Analysis (TDMEA), a novel methodology adapted from Failure Mode and Effect Analysis (FMEA), designed to proactively identify, analyze, and prioritize potential trust failures in edge AI. We highlight that inherent edge limitations (e.g., computational power, memory, energy) exacerbate complex trade-offs among critical trust dimensions such as accuracy, privacy, and explainability. TDMEA provides a structured approach to dissecting these challenges, emphasizing the critical role of detectability in prioritizing risks given edge monitoring limitations. The framework underscores that trust degradation is an evolving problem, driven by dynamic environments and necessary edge optimizations. TDMEA time dependency can provide real time index which can be explored further as an AI measure index. We conclude that building resilient trust in edge AI requires a holistic design philosophy, integrating resource-efficient robustness, lightweight adaptive learning, privacy-preserving solutions, and on-device self-healing, all guided by continuous governance and monitoring. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Resource Constraint Edge; Trust Degradation Mode; Trustworthy},
	keywords = {Privacy by design; Privacy-preserving techniques; Safety engineering; Analysis frameworks; Computational power; Effects analysis; Failure mode and effects analysis; Mode effects; Novel methodology; Resource Constraint; Resource constraint edge; Trust degradation mode; Trustworthy; Economic and social effects},
