	affiliations = {Department of Economics, Faculty of Economics, Hannan University, Osaka, Japan},
	abstract = {The use of AI technology is expected to dramatically improve the efficiency of the entire supply chain as well as the realization of smart factories. This chapter focuses on several key topics regarding the implementation of AI technology in the rapidly developing chemical industry, and outlines research trends with a focus on previous studies from 2020 to 2024, mainly. Big data analytics with AI and ML will improve efficiency with respect to forecasting and accuracy of highly uncertain demand and tracking and visualizing inventory management; building logistics networks based on AI-driven inventory management and stock-out prevention increases efficiency throughout the supply chain and enables performance assessment across suppliers. Automated management in production scheduling, quality control, and inventory replenishment drives efficiencies throughout the supply chain. Improved forecasting accuracy enhances risk management performance, including automated responses to difficult-to-foresee events. The technologies needed to realize such an AI-driven supply chain are being developed through the validation of various models and methods, the identification of parameters that can be used to improve performance, and the comparative evaluation of model performance. However, there are many technical issues related to risk management and risk prediction that require further progress. Ethical standards for AI use are in flux, and from the perspective of corporate social responsibility, promoting transparency of internal ethical rules and accountability for AI use will help manage risk in the future. The use of these AI technologies is also expected to be applied to efforts related to the decarbonization of chemical industrial zones. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
