	affiliations = {College of Smart Agriculture, College of Artificial Intelligence, Nanjing Agricultural University, Nanjing, China; College of Economics and Management, Nanjing Agricultural University, Nanjing, China},
	abstract = {To address the functional safety challenges stemming from the inherent fragility of artificial intelligence (AI) algorithms in Unmanned Vehicle, this paper introduces a digital twin-based adversarial testing platform. The platform features an innovative three-layer Physical-Twin-Enhancement architecture. Its core component, the Enhancement Layer, decomposes adversarial generation into behavioral and perceptual modalities, leveraging dedicated generative models to systematically construct highrisk, safety-critical scenarios. Validation experiments demonstrate the platform's efficacy in uncovering latent vulnerabilities. In behavioral modality tests, the generated adversarial traffic flow increased the Scenario Collision Rate (SCR) for the tested decision-making algorithm (VectorNet) from 0% to 38.5%, while the minimum Time-To-Collision (Min-TTC) was drastically reduced from 4.2 s to 0.8 s. Concurrently, in perceptual modality tests, adversarial textures degraded the performance of the perception model (YOLOv5), causing its average detection confidence to decrease from 0.92 to 0.31 and its precision to fall from 94.5% to 35.2%. The platform operates on a closedloop workflow encompassing state perception, twin mapping, adversarial enhancement, and virtual injection. This process facilitates the systematic evolution of test cases from standard conditions to boundary-risk scenarios, offering a comprehensive solution for the rigorous evaluation and enhancement of AI safety in autonomous systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
