	abstract = {With the expansion of e-commerce, traditional customer service has transformed extensively due to generative AI. This paper underscores how advanced generation messaging mechanism can transcend basic automation by incorporating emotional intelligence, linguistic adaptability, and reliability - three crucial aspects of efficient interaction. By doing a survey-based analysis along with employing a theoretical model, we attempt to examine how large language models (LLMs) can be improved with the ability to recognize intent and emotional nuances, as well as generate responses in multiple languages. Such an improvement can led to the creation of more tailored, empathetic, and inclusive support experiences for users. It has been argued that trust, in the generative AI age, is not a mere feature - it is the foundationThe paper aims to propose a hybrid AI-human messaging framework that potentially adapts to the emotional condition of the users, their linguistic preferences, and other commitments. Sentiment classifiers and multilingual NLP models are employed by the system to guarantee culturally appropriate and emotionally sound responses. Moreover, the system also escalates crucial or sensitive interactions to humans. Besides, the paper focuses on ethical considerations including as user transparency, data sensitivity, and the disclosure of AI involvement in customer communications. The key findings of the research unveil an astounding fact that the customers or users seek fluency in communication, expecting that the messaging system would easily comprehend their tone, interact in their native language, and comply with human ethical norms. Through a multidisciplinary approach encompassing insights from interactive AI, HCI, and AI ethics, the paper advocates for a better human-centered prospect in AI to improve customer support. © 2025 IEEE.},
