	abstract = {The increasing digitalization of power systems has transformed the smart grid into a cyber-physical ecosystem, making it highly vulnerable to cyber-attacks. Intrusion detection systems (IDS's) play a major role in protecting the smart grid infrastructure. But common machine learning and deep learning techniques, being, in many respects, black boxes, are not readily applicable to the fields requiring safety. In recent years, explainable AI (XAI) has become a very interesting new solution for improving interpretability, trust, and accountability of IDS models. In this paper, an extensive survey of the current trends in intrusion detection for smart grid systems using explainable AI techniques is presented. This study categorizes recent approaches based on model families, interpretability methods, and application contexts, highlighting their strengths, limitations, and suitability for real-time deployment. Furthermore, this study examines benchmark datasets commonly used for evaluation, discusses open challenges such as scalability, adversarial robustness, and identifies promising research directions. Overall, the study aims to give researchers and practitioners better information in finding trustworthy and efficient IDS solutions for future smart grid operation.  © 2025 IEEE.},
