	affiliations = {Jaipur National University, School of Computer and System Sciences, Jaipur, India},
	abstract = {In rural economies, particularly in developing regions like India, two urgent and interlinked challenges persist: limited access to formal credit due to "thin-file"consumer profiles and insufficient monitoring of soil organic carbon (SOC), critical for climate-resilient agriculture. This paper proposes a novel dual-task machine learning framework that unifies credit scoring and SOC classification using a synthetic dataset comprising 5,000 records simulating rural households. The dataset integrates demographic, behavioral, psychometric, agronomic, and remote sensing features such as NDVI, LST, and rainfall anomalies, inspired by real-world data sources including SoilGrids250m and Sentinel-2 [10], [23].We develop a multi-task learning (MTL) architecture using XGBoost and Random Forests to simultaneously predict creditworthiness and SOC classes. Model explainability is ensured via SHAP values, revealing key cross-domain predictors like mobile transaction frequency, rainfall intensity, and cognitive resilience scores [19], [30]. Experimental results demonstrate high performance in both tasks, achieving an F1-score of 0.82 for credit risk prediction and a macro-average accuracy of 0.78 for SOC classification. Fairness and model bias are evaluated using group fairness indices aligned with ESG and Responsible AI frameworks [18], [29], [33].This study also introduces the Unified Rural Credit & Carbon Dataset [8], an openly accessible synthetic dataset designed for reproducibility and policy experimentation, linked to the author's Ph.D. thesis on thin-file credit scoring [6], [7]. The proposed framework addresses Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) 1, 13, and 9 by offering scalable, ethical AI-based decision tools for inclusive finance and climate adaptation in data-scarce regions. The convergence of financial inclusion and carbon intelligence provides a new paradigm for ESG-aligned development in rural economies [9], [13], [22]. © 2025 IEEE.},
