	affiliations = {Vel Tech Rangarajan Dr. Sagunthala R&D Institute of Science and Technology, Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, Avadi, Chennai, 600062, India},
	abstract = {In automatic classification of skin illnesses using thermoscopic and clinical photos, deep learning (DL) has shown remarkable performance, frequently outperforming dermatologists in accuracy. These models, sometimes known as "black-box"systems, are opaque, which raises serious questions about their acceptability, openness, and dependability in therapeutic settings. The goal of this research is to close the gap between reliable medical decision-making and high-performance AI by presenting an explainable deep learning (XDL) framework for skin disease identification. In order to visualize and interpret model predictions, the suggested framework combines post-hoc explainability techniques like Grad-CAM and LIME with convolutional neural networks (CNNs) for skin lesion categorization. Clinicians can verify AI outputs against established dermatological criteria by using the system to highlight discriminative picture regions that contribute to particular diagnosis. Benchmark data sets, such as HAM 10000 and ISIC2020, which cover several classifications like malignance, basal cell carcinoma, and benign keratosis, are used to assess the framework. The XDL model maintains interpretability using visual explanation maps while achieving over 92% classification accuracy, according to the results. Furthermore, we present a clinician feedback loop that allows for model improvement through user-in-the-loop learning, gradually increasing accuracy and confidence. Scalable integration into tele dermatology platforms is made possible by the system's optimization for deployment in cloud and edge environments. This method solves ethical and practical deployment issues while paving the way for transparent, clinician-friendly, and regulatory-compliant AI systems for dermatology. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs); Deep Learning; Dermatology Automation; Explainable AI (XAI); Grad-CAM; Medical Image Analysis; Skin Disease Detection; Trustworthy AI},
