	abstract = {Long reliant on imported arms, Gulf Co-operation Council (GCC) states are pursuing ambitious strategies to localise defence production and build technological sovereignty. This article argues that their prospects are best understood through the lens of New Defence–a framework defined by the spin-in of civilian technologies, software-defined engineering, and private financing models. After outlining these structural features, the article examines GCC initiatives in space and artificial intelligence, two dual-use sectors where Gulf investments are most advanced. The analysis shows that whilst GCC projects strongly align with civilian technology appropriation, AI, and software-defined innovation, they remain heavily dependent on state-led financing and lack operational feedback loops. The paper concludes that institutional reform and integration into global innovation cycles are essential for success. © 2025 The Author(s). Published by Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.},
