	abstract = {Healthcare services now completely utilize technology, especially artificial intelligence (AI). This study focuses on the following important areas and is founded on a general literature evaluation that reveals the significance of AI in healthcare. Among the acknowledged application areas are the following: (1) diagnostics and medical imaging; (2) e-patients and telemedicine; health and medical research, consumer health, rehabilitation, and more are included in (3) and (4). AI is being used in various ways, including diagnosis and medical imaging, diagnosis and management of the 2019 coronavirus (COVID-19), virtual care, document management, patient interaction and treatment regimen compliance, workload reduction for healthcare professionals, the discovery of new drugs and vaccines, prescription error checking, records and data, and treatment enhancement. Thus privacy, safety, the right to choose and experiment, prices, information and consent, access difficulties, and the efficacy of integrating AI into healthcare services are among the technological, ethical, and social issues raised by this research presentation. In particular, it has a suitable effect on patient safety and  healthcare professionals (HCP) certification, which is why it is required to increase HCPs’ confidence in enhancing acceptability and significant health results. These factors imply that, with the correct kind of governance, it is feasible to strengthen the adoption and application of AI while also making the necessary thoughtful adjustments to the ethical, legal, and trust issues. The concept of AI has contributed to a change in health systems when COVID-19 attacked the global health system, and perhaps such an uprising could help to steer future health needs. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
