	abstract = {The concept of artificial intelligence (AI) is ever developing and infiltrating into the most essential fields, including businesses and intelligent houses, thus making the issues of privacy, security, and trustworthiness even more relevant. In this respect, the current research presents a new privacy-saving AI framework that will handle these issues without violating the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF). The suggested framework integrates in a synergistic manner, the use of differential privacy, federated learning and reinforcement learning to guarantee data confidentiality as well as real-time detection of anomaly under dynamic environments. Placing these methods in an integrated architecture, the framework offers a high level of protection against data leakage and inference attacks with minimal loss of model performance. We discuss the adoption of this framework by enterprise networks and smart home systems and show how it can create a robust and responsive analytics platform that can run in highly diverse device ecosystems. With the modular design, there is easy integration of new data sources and learning paradigms that increase the operational scalability. As evidenced by empirical testing, the model achieves great detection rates, significantly low false-positive rates and better adjustment to changing threat environments. These performance indicators highlight the possibility of the framework to ensure high-quality security standards without interfering with analytical effectiveness. The paper also explains how the NIST AI RMF can be used to manage the risk management process across the AI lifecycle in such a way that the privacy and trustworthiness principles are maintained without adversely affecting the overall performance of the system. This congruence supports the conformance of this framework to the new regulatory demands. Lastly, extensive tests on benchmark datasets confirm the framework to be effective in practice deployment cases, which proves its relevance in practice and strong performance in a wide variety of operational settings. © 2025 IEEE.},
