	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032842241?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Kalinga University, Chhattisgarh, Naya Raipur, India},
	abstract = {The rapid growth of smart home technology has given rise to the possibility of a new generation of human-centric automation, where human convenience and intelligent decisionmaking are of paramount importance. In this paper, an NLPbased Artificial Intelligence framework is presented for home automation, aiming to enhance user experience by enabling simple voice- and text-based interactions with home appliances. In contrast to conventional rule-based systems, the proposed effort here couples cutting-edge NLP methods with deep learning methods for the automatic detection and handling of user intent in real-time, as well as the effortless management of lighting, HVAC, security devices, and entertainment systems. The architecture combines four central modules: (1) speech/text input and preprocessing, (2) intent identification via pre-trained language models (e.g., BERT or GPT), (3) contextdependent decision-making via reinforcement learning, and (4) an action layer which communicates with IoT-capable home appliances. The system learns user behavior incrementally with ongoing learning, developing customized automation routines and dynamically assigning values based on user command and contextual information (e.g., time, occupancy, and weather). A case study deployment illustrates the responsiveness and efficacy of the system in a proof-of-concept smart home setup. The architecture provides greater than 92% intent classification accuracy and orders-of-magnitude latency reduction in command execution compared to traditional systems. The model also incorporates privacy-preserving designs and can run within a hybrid cloud-edge infrastructure, ensuring data security and reliability. The outcomes show that NLP-supported smart home infrastructures not only enhance the user interaction with natural language but also offer the potential for even more intelligent and responsive living environments. Future advancements can involve emotional perception and multimodal input (e.g., facial expressions, gestures) to achieve even more humanized interaction. © 2025 IEEE.},
