	title = {Algorithmic Echo Chambers and user Interaction in Understanding the Societal Impact of Personalized Content},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {2025 MIPRO 48th ICT and Electronics Convention, MIPRO 2025 - Proceedings},
	pages = {1477 - 1483},
	doi = {10.1109/MIPRO65660.2025.11132046},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105016672006?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Centre for Defence and Strategic Studies "Janko Bobetko", Zagreb, Croatia},
	abstract = {This study examines how algorithmic echo chambers impact user interactions and societal perceptions through personalized content on social media platforms. It focuses on human-computer interaction dynamics, highlighting how algorithms amplify emotionally engaging content, reinforce existing beliefs, and marginalize critical perspectives, contributing to polarization and decreasing exposure to diverse viewpoints. Using case studies of TikTok, Instagram, and Facebook, the research demonstrates that algorithmic systems prioritize engagement metrics over inclusivity. Through discourse analysis and algorithmic simulations, it reveals that echo chambers create ideological silos, limiting meaningful dialogue. The findings indicate that focusing on emotionally charged narratives significantly strengthens confirmation bias. The study suggests interventions such as algorithmic audits, counter-narratives, and efforts to diversify content exposure to address polarization. Additionally, it provides practical recommendations for designing transparent and usercentered digital platforms that balance engagement with ethical considerations, including fostering algorithmic transparency and promoting content diversity. By addressing the societal implications of personalized content, this research contributes to developing ethical interaction frameworks for social media, advancing both the technological and societal dimensions of human-computer interaction. The Algorithmic Accountability and Communication Model (AACM) offers a theoretical foundation for understanding the relationship between algorithms, user interactions, and societal impacts, bridging classic communication theories with contemporary challenges. © 2025 IEEE.},
