	affiliations = {TX, United States; WV, United States},
	abstract = {In the evolving digital workplace, human capital management (HCM) systems increasingly rely on artificial intelligence to enhance employee engagement, automate HR functions, and support decision-making. However, the integration of Conversational AI in HCM raises challenges related to ethics, transparency, and human-centric design. This study addresses the problem of developing an ethical and intelligent conversational framework for Workday HCM that balances automation efficiency with user trust and inclusivity. To major objective is to design a human-centered digital assistant capable of ethical decision-making, contextual understanding, and adaptive communication across diverse workforce scenarios. The Transformer architecture is used for high-dimensional feature extraction from textual and interactional data, followed by multi-objective swarm optimization (MOSO) to select optimal feature subsets. The fuzzy conversational deep neural network (F-CDNN) is used to capture uncertain and context-dependent user intents, ensuring adaptability and fairness in conversational flow. The study employs enterprise-level recruitment and financial data from the RESSET Chinese enterprise big data platform and the CSMAR database (2016-2023) to validate model performance and ethical robustness. Experimental findings demonstrate that the proposed Conversational AI significantly improves dialogue accuracy, ethical compliance, and user satisfaction while reducing bias and misinterpretation compared to existing HCM assistants. This work contributes to the emerging discourse on responsible AI in workplace systems by providing a scalable, interpretable, and human-aligned digital assistant framework for next-generation HCM. © 2025 IEEE.},
