	affiliations = {University of Salerno, Dept. Computer Science, Saler, Fisciano, Italy},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI) has become a cornerstone of modern healthcare, yet its deployment raises critical concerns about energy efficiency and environmental impact. While much research emphasizes accuracy and training optimization, inference-time sustainability remains largely overlooked despite being the most frequent and resource-intensive phase in clinical use. In this work, we present a sustainable AI framework for ECG-based biometric identification that explicitly targets inference efficiency. Using the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia dataset, we trained a baseline CNN (CardioIdNet) that achieved 98.2% accuracy, 98.2% precision, recall, and F1-score, with an AUC of 99.9%. To reduce its computational footprint, we applied knowledge distillation and post-training quantization. The distilled student model preserved almost all predictive performance (96.9% accuracy) while reducing model size by 82%, inference latency by 56%, and energy consumption by 66%. The quantized model minimized CPU load and memory footprint but revealed hardware-dependent sustainability trade-offs. All models were assessed across performance, energy, carbon emissions (via ISPRA-certified factors), and computational load. Our results provide a reproducible pipeline for deploying reliable and environmentally responsible AI in healthcare, aligning ECG biometrics with the broader paradigm of Green AI. © 2025 IEEE.},
