	affiliations = {Wong & Co, United States; The George Washington University Law School, United States},
	abstract = {“AI judges” refers to the transformative opportunity for artificial intelligence (AI), including generative AI tools, in legal decision-making. This entry explores AI's recent integration into judicial and arbitral decision-making globally through both procedural innovations and advancements in how courts and arbitral tribunals may render substantive decisions. Several recent case studies provide useful examples, including assistance by ChatGPT-4 in Pakistani court proceedings, AI-assisted courts in China, and an AI arbitration assistant in Guangzhou Province, China. This entry assesses AI's potential and challenges in judicial and arbitral decision-making, particularly regarding transparency, ethical considerations, and the balance between technological efficiency and human oversight. It analyses transparency obligations for the use of AI in judicial and arbitral decision-making. It also explores emerging rights to contest AI-driven legal decisions, advocating for responsible AI integration to uphold fairness and trust in judicial outcomes. © The Editors and Contributors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.},
