	abstract = {Mechanistic interpretability is a research subfield of artificial intelligence focused on understanding the internal mechanisms of neural networks. Unlike traditional interpretability approaches that analyze input-output relationships, mechanistic interpretability seeks to reverse engineer models by identifying computational circuits, examining data representations, and conducting causal interventions. The field has evolved from early visualization techniques to systematic methodologies for analyzing artificial neurons, layers, and information flow. Recent advancements in mechanistic interpretability have been driven by its applications in AI safety, model development, and performance optimization. Key challenges include scaling analysis methods, addressing emergent behaviors, and standardizing research methodologies. As mechanistic interpretability progresses, it is expected to contribute significantly to AI transparency, reliability, and ethical deployment. © The Editors and Contributors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.},
