	pages = {},
	doi = {10.2118/229682-MS},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033749729?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Maged Mabrook ADNOC, United Arab Emirates; InceptionAI/G42, United Arab Emirates},
	abstract = {The upstream oil and gas (O&G) sector is at a critical inflection point. While past decades of automation and digitalization have significantly optimized workflows, they still relied heavily on human oversight. Today, the emergence of Agentic AI marks a transformative shift-enabling intelligent agents to independently execute complex tasks, make context-aware decisions, and dynamically adapt to real-time operational data, significantly reducing the need for constant human involvement. Unlike earlier incremental advances, Agentic AI represents a fundamental disruption. These autonomous systems integrate technologies such as large language models (LLMs) with decades of domain-specific data, enabling capabilities far beyond traditional automation. This paper explores the transformative potential of Agentic AI through the lens of ENERGYai-a novel AI framework purpose-built for upstream applications. We examine how Agentic AI is being implemented across critical upstream workflows-from seismic interpretation and reservoir modeling to drilling optimization and production management. ENERGYai, for instance, has delivered seismic analysis up to 80% faster, increased production efficiency by as much as 15%, and reduced unplanned downtime through proactive asset monitoring and intervention.Interestingly, digitally native junior professionals are seeing disproportionate productivity gains, as Agentic AI augments their domain knowledge with real-time insights and decision support. This shift is not just about technology-it's about workforce transformation. However, key challenges remain ensuring data quality, implementing robust QA/QC processes, and, crucially, building trust in autonomous decision-making systems. Change management is essential. Successful adoption requires not only technical integration but also cultural readiness. Organizations that empower agile, AI-literate talent, embrace open data standards, and shift away from restrictive legacy systems and licensing models will be best positioned to lead this next era of upstream innovation. In conclusion, Agentic AI redefines how upstream operations are conceived and executed. It offers a step-change in efficiency, responsiveness, and innovation. Companies that move decisively to adopt these technologies will unlock significant competitive advantages-while those that resist risk being left behind in a rapidly evolving, AI-driven industry landscape. Copyright © 2025, Society of Petroleum Engineers.},
	keywords = {Competition; Data reduction; Decision making; Gasoline; Legacy systems; Metadata; Personnel; Petroleum reservoir evaluation; Seismology; Complex task; Context-Aware; Domain specific; Human oversight; Inflexion point; Integrate technologies; Language model; Oil and Gas Sector; Real time operational data; Work-flows; Decision support systems},
