	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105016558876?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Management, Seattle University, United States},
	abstract = {The claim that cultures do not share the same values is uncontroversial, although the sense in which that statement is true is poorly understood. Cultures actually do have much in common concerning which general values are important but differ in how they emphasize and interpret those values. This creates significant differences at the level of cultural norms. Insofar as the United States is a main hub of artificial intelligence research and development, it is particularly important which values are emphasized and how they are interpreted in an American context, and therefore, what norms are created. Among these are emphases on bias, privacy, and existential risk. These three norms in their particular interpretations dominate the landscape of artificial intelligence (AI) ethics in the US. The purpose of this chapter is to understand how these norms around AI developed and how they are currently interpreted in the context of AI ethics research. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Naresh Singh and Ram B. Ramachandran; individual chapters, the contributors.},
