	affiliations = {Al-Turath University, Baghdad, Iraq; Al-Mansour University College, Baghdad, Iraq; Osh State University, Osh, Kyrgyzstan; Al-Rafidain University College, Baghdad, Iraq; Madenat Alelem University College, Baghdad, Iraq},
	abstract = {With AI systems being integrated into processes at an unprecedented pace, many have raised concerns as to what this means for both operational efficiency and legal compliance across various industries, particularly within the textile manufacturing sector. This study explores the impact of AI technologies prevalence on production performance, quality control, and compliance with international trade and regulatory frameworks among textile manufacturers in North America, Europe, and Asia. By utilizing a multifaceted approach comprising industrial surveys, operational data analysis, and legal expert interviews, the research examines AI’s contribution to curbing defects, expediting production processes, conserving energy, and aiding in the adherence to standards like ISO 9001, OEKO-TEX, and WTO trade standards. Generalized linear modeling and variance decomposition were used to assess the relationship between AI maturity and regulatory outcomes. The study found that AI implementation resulted in measurable improvements in operational metrics and legal accountability when embedded throughout enterprise functions. Firms that are more evolved in their AI integration process obtain higher compliance scores and face less regulatory risks, according to the study. Such tight audit trails and tracking over time basically confirm the legal defensibility and sustainability of the processes powered by the AI. It reveals uneven adoption across companies of different sizes and regions, and demonstrates the need to move towards scalable, inclusive AI strategies and harmonized legal standards. © 2025, Ivanovo State Polytechnic University. All rights reserved.},
