	abstract = {This chapter provides an in-depth examination of how AI is reshaping the foundations of democratic processes in the digital age. Focusing on the integration of AI into electoral systems, it explores the transformative potential and inherent tensions that arise when data-driven technologies intersect with participatory governance. The discussion centers on the deployment of predictive analytics to interpret voter behavior, the enhancement of fraud detection in elections, and the use of AI to inform public policy across varied political landscapes. Drawing on comparative case studies from countries such as India and the United States, the chapter highlights real-world applications of AI in election monitoring, combating misinformation, and strengthening electoral transparency. This chapter also considers the emerging use of AI to foster civic engagement, personalize political campaign strategies, and support inclusive policy development. Crucially, the chapter interrogates the ethical, legal, and institutional challenges posed by AI in democratic settings, especially in contexts where democratic norms are fragile or contested. By critically assessing existing tools and governance frameworks, it proposes actionable recommendations and ethical guidelines to safeguard democratic integrity. The chapter concludes by reflecting on the dual-edged trajectory of AI in democracy—its capacity to empower civic participation and bolster democratic resilience, and its potential for manipulation and control in authoritarian regimes—calling for a globally informed, rights-based approach to AI governance in democratic societies. © 2026 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.},
