	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105009222778?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {CyberWorx Energy, Washington, DC, United States},
	abstract = {Emerging technologies such as Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) are transforming the energy sector by enhancing operational efficiency, predictive maintenance, and grid optimization. However, as AI-driven automation becomes integral to energy infrastructure, it also introduces significant cybersecurity vulnerabilities. The dual nature of AI, as both a powerful tool for efficiency and a potential liability, necessitates a robust cybersecurity framework to mitigate risks associated with its deployment. The research presented in this paper adopts a two-pronged approach: (1) it examines the cybersecurity frameworks currently employed in the energy sector, with a focus on safeguarding AI-driven operations with particular attention given to the vulnerabilities present in large language models (LLMs) and other AI-based infrastructures; (2) it evaluates regulatory frameworks in the U.S., assessing their effectiveness in governing AI, addressing emerging threats, and ensuring data integrity. This includes an analysis of existing risk management tools and ethical considerations essential for creating a secure AI environment in the energy industry. The findings reveal critical gaps in existing cybersecurity policies that expose AI systems to adversarial threats, potentially compromising operational reliability and data security. To address these risks, a comprehensive cybersecurity strategy is needed to enhance AI system protection through transparency, explainability, and accountability. This strategy balances the rapid adoption of AI innovations with secure operational practices, ensuring that GEN AI deployment in the energy sector remains resilient against cyber threats. Additionally, emerging regulatory trends and technological solutions for enhancing AI security show that the role of quantum computing within the energy sector poses not only opportunities but also challenges/dangers. The necessity of quantum-resistant encryption methods is discussed to counteract potential future threats posed by quantum-enabled cyberattacks. Lastly, real-world case studies illustrate the practical implementation of these cybersecurity measures, demonstrating both the risks associated with AI adoption and the strategies that can be employed to mitigate them. Ultimately, the proposed frameworks aim to promote secure, transparent, and accountable AI usage in the energy sector. By addressing cybersecurity vulnerabilities proactively, this research underscores the importance of comprehensive governance frameworks in future-proofing AI infrastructure against evolving cyber threats. Copyright 2025, Society of Petroleum Engineers.},
	keywords = {Artificial intelligence; Crime; Cybersecurity; Energy policy; Learning systems; Network security; Petroleum engineering; Predictive maintenance; Quantum computers; Quantum cryptography; Risk analysis; Risk management; Artificial intelligence learning; Artificial intelligence systems; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Cyber-attacks; Emerging technologies; Energy sector; Machine-learning; Operational efficiencies; Predictive maintenance; Risk assessment},
