	abstract = {The existing copyright laws struggle in terms of relevance to emerging technologies like generative AI. The purpose of copyright laws was to protect artistic and expressive works and give creators control over the distribution and adaptation of their work. Generative AI uses massive datasets (often without permission) for training and, through training, can reproduce outputs inspired by the datasets, suggesting infringement of copyright. The increasing use of data colonialism, a method of acquisition of private data in the purview of governments, non-governmental organizations, and corporations, is creating further complications both at national and international levels. These data flows across borders and companies require a global AI governance outlook. Anticipatory governance in this space needs a stress test to see if existing laws and frameworks are sufficient or being force fit into a new context and, if the latter, needs to be able to buy time to change the regulations. © 2025 by World Scientific Publishing Co. Pte. Ltd. All rights reserved.},
