	pages = {153 - 164},
	doi = {10.52202/083075-0022},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031887510?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Airbus Defence and Space, GmbH, Immenstaad am Bodensee, 88090, Germany; Starlab Space, LLC, Webster, 77598, TX, United States; Palantir Technologies, 1200 17th Street, Denver, 80202, CO, United States; The Ohio State University, Columbus, 43210, OH, United States},
	abstract = {The Starlab program is a global collaboration ensuring continuous astronaut presence in low-Earth orbit (LEO) while advancing microgravity research and commercialization. Starlab is designed to support national space agencies, private enterprises, universities, and non-profits, offering a versatile platform for scientific discovery and technological innovation. This paper presents an overview of the Starlab payload system, which includes internal and external capabilities, customer payload integration, and the operational concept that enables autonomous and efficient research. The Internal Payload Laboratory provides essential infrastructure for a wide range of research needs, including cold stowage incubators, temporary stowage, and advanced data acquisition tools such as microscopes, plate readers, and molecular genetic processing systems. The External Payload Laboratory offers a multidimensional research environment outside the station with multi-dimensional views of both Earth and space, as well as exposure to hard-vacuum and the radiation environment of LEO, expanding experimental possibilities. A key differentiator of Starlab is its artificial intelligence (AI)-driven approach, integrated from the design phase. Traditional space operations depend on manual processes and basic machine learning systems that limit operational independence. Starlab will break this paradigm by implementing an advanced AI-driven architecture using Palantir's Artificial Intelligence Platform and cutting-edge generative AI technologies. This will enable Earth-independent operations with automated decision-making, reducing human oversight to the minimum. Designed as a next-generation research platform, Starlab's adaptive AI framework will anticipate and accommodate emerging technological innovations. This will ensure operational resilience and experimental adaptability as scientific methodologies evolve. The platform will operate through AI-primary systems with crew oversight, establishing the technological foundation for autonomous microgravity research in LEO. Copyright © 2025 by the International Astronautical Federation (IAF). All rights reserved.},
