	affiliations = {Engineering and Computing College, Information Technology Departmen LIWA University, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates},
	abstract = {Many higher education students struggle to meet academic expectations, directly affecting their institutional progress and hindering the fulfillment of Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 4. This goal emphasizes inclusive education and lifelong learning, ensuring that students develop essential competencies for personal and professional success. However, traditional academic support systems often rely on static, retrospective data, limiting their ability to provide real-time interventions and adapt to students’ evolving needs. Struggling students may go unnoticed without proactive measures until their performance declines significantly. To address these challenges, this research introduces an artificial intelligence (AI)-driven monitoring and risk management (MRM) system, which leverages adaptive AI algorithms to identify at-risk students before academic difficulties escalate. The system uses predictive analytics to assess student performance trends and provides personalized recommendations that empower educators to implement timely, targeted interventions. This proactive approach ensures that students receive the necessary support to stay on track academically, ultimately enhancing institutional efficiency in managing student outcomes. The proposed methodology employs a hybrid AI framework, combining neural networks for high-accuracy predictions with decision trees for greater interpretability. This balance allows educators and decision-makers to trust AI-generated insights, ensuring that predictions are precise and transparent. A case study conducted in UAE higher education institutions evaluates the system’s effectiveness in improving student performance through real-time feedback mechanisms and adaptive instructional strategies while optimizing institutional resource allocation. Additionally, the scalability and adaptability of the MRM system enable it to align with institutional goals, promoting continuous learning and efficient resource utilization. By tackling key challenges in student performance monitoring and institutional decision-making, this research contributes to integrating AI into education, supporting the realization of SDG 4, and advancing the role of intelligent systems in sustainable academic development. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Suneeta Satpathy, Álvaro Rocha, Sachi Nandan Mohanty and Tanupriya Choudhury individual chapters, the contributors.},
