	doi = {10.1109/PuneCon67554.2025.11378710},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034664289?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Chandigarh University, Punjab, Mohali, India},
	abstract = {Thus, there are great potential advantages of the constant development of new artificial intelligence (AI) technologies to save and, subsequently, reconstruct endangered indigenous languages, many of which are now threatened by extinction due to globalization and the prevalence of the major world languages. New approaches for documentation, translation, and education in spoken and written languages can be applied by using AI-based tools, language models, and machine translation systems to help document and translate these languages. Nonetheless, potential problems of applying AI to language documentation are just as important, such as ownership, data privacy, cultural and social bias, and possibilities of exploitation. In this paper, we investigate the potential risks and management issues related to the deployment of artificial intelligence in archiving threatened indigenous languages. We explore the use of VGG11 and VGG16, which are primarily image recognition models typically used for tasks such as visual data analysis, including the recognition of handwritten texts in endangered languages. While VGG11 and VGG16 are leveraged in this context for analyzing visual data, the focus is on AI's broader contribution to language documentation and preservation. We also examine both the potential benefits and risks of AI for indigenous languages, particularly emphasizing the importance of community-driven strategies. Ensuring inclusive benefits, honoring regional traditions, and establishing accountable management frameworks for AI implementation are crucial issues. To support linguistic diversity, this article advocates for a balanced approach to AI in language preservation, emphasizing sustainable models and ethical governance. © 2025 IEEE.},
