	abstract = {The adoption of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the oil and gas sector is revolutionizing monitoring, predictive maintenance, and environmental sustainability. The research examines how AI is streamlining operational efficiency, safety, and sustainability objectives. Through a quantitative research design, questionnaires were administered to industry experts, such as engineers, data analysts, and operations managers, to determine the impact of AI. Results indicate that predictive maintenance using AI reduces downtime by up to 25% and also reduces maintenance costs by 20-30%, which dramatically improves the reliability of assets. Further, AI streamlines resources through processing big data to limit wastage and boost output. Tracking mechanisms based on AI allow for real-time leak and emission detection, reducing greenhouse gas emissions by up to 40% and making it easier to comply with environmental policy. Despite these benefits, workforce resistance, high costs of implementation, and cybersecurity risks are some of the key challenges to AI adoption. The study also demands strategic investment in AI infrastructure, education of the workforce, and regulatory support to facilitate smoother adoption. Long-term trends of AI adoption, cross-industry comparisons, and the role of AI in sustainable innovation must be dealt with in future studies. By resolving these challenges, AI has the potential to unleash efficiency, cost savings, and environmental sustainability, positioning the oil and gas industry for a more sustainable future.  © 2025 IEEE.},
