	affiliations = {Institute for Management Research, Civitas University, Poland},
	abstract = {This chapter examines aesthetic insights into human-AI collaboration within organisational and managerial contexts within the humanistic management paradigm. While AI is often associated with efficiency and automation, its role in humanistic management remains underexplored. This chapter addresses this research gap by investigating how AI can impact aesthetic perception and humanistic values in varied organisational contexts. Based on a literature review of management studies, AI, aesthetics, philosophy, and psychology, the analysis covers historical perspectives on aesthetic insight, emerging AI appliances in managerial practice, and the impact on humanism preservation. The key results may be summarised as follows: (1) AI may influence corporate storytelling, branding, leadership communication, and workplace aesthetics, offering both creative augmentation and risks of standardisation; (2) AI’s role in managerial decision-making can optimise efficiency but risks homogenisation, bias reinforcement, and the loss of creative intuition; (3) AI should be treated as a co-creative tool, supporting human aesthetic intuition while maintaining ethical oversight and inclusivity. The findings suggest that while AI may enhance leadership aesthetic perception and organisational identity, over-reliance on AI can lead to dehumanisation and cultural standardisation within organisational contexts and managerial practice. This chapter may be relevant to scholars in management, AI ethics, organisational aesthetics, and digital humanities, as well as business practitioners and policymakers seeking to integrate AI-powered solutions into a human-centred managerial paradigm. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Michał Szostak; individual chapters, the contributors.},
