	abstract = {Understanding and predicting long-term college outcomes plays a vital role in the assurance of a fair policy making system and the empowerment of public stakeholders. Yet, current strategies fail to achieve a simultaneous balance of high predictive performance, interpretability and demographic fairness. This paper introduces a fair machine learning pipeline based on the U.S. College Scorecard data to predict median 10-year post-entry earnings, four-year graduation rates, and median student debt of 1,988 U.S. colleges. In this study, Random Forest (baseline) and XGBoost regression models are implemented, supported by a comprehensive preprocessing process involving median imputation, one-hot encoding and generalization of pairwise interaction terms. Hyperparameter optimization is conducted via randomized search combined with a 3-fold cross-validation. XGBoost achieves R<sup>2</sup> scores of 0.631, 0.639, and 0.627 for earnings, graduation rates, and debt, respectively and outperforms Random Forest baselines by up to 15% in the reduction of mean squared error. Fairlearn's MetricFrame audit reveals severe disparities with as much as 20x increased prediction errors in certain regions and biases against for-profit institutions and specific ethnic/racial groups. A SHAP analysis identifies SAT/ACT scores, family income, tuition costs, and demographic factors are recognized as crucial predicting factors. Although tree-based models deliver strong accuracy, fairness-aware approaches are needed to address persistent inequities and ensure ethical educational analytics. © 2025 IEEE.},
