	affiliations = {Sr University, School of Business, Telangana, Warangal, 506371, India},
	abstract = {The increasing application of AI-driven decision-making in employee recruitment and talent management has catalyzed widespread concerns regarding algorithmic fairness and bias. Most traditional machine learning models learn from existing biased training data, leading to discriminatory recruitment, reduced workforce diversity, and ethical concerns. This paper proposes an adaptive and explainable generative adversarial network framework that is fair, maintains high prediction accuracy, and is extremely interpretable. In this work, the framework utilizes GANs to create synthetic hiring data that are fair and unbiased, thus enhancing model generalization and enabling fair recruitment. It also utilizes explainable AI techniques, such as SHapley Additive exPlanations-based feature attribution, for establishing transparency and trust in AI-driven human resource systems. A fairness-sensitive optimization goal is employed to counter disparate impact, which guarantees equitable hiring rates across demographic groups. A comparative evaluation with baseline models, such as neural networks, logistic regression, and decision trees, shows that the proposed framework reaches better accuracy of 88%, increased fairness, and increased interpretability. An adaptive learning module refreshes the decision boundaries dynamically, preventing their bias accumulation with time. Experimental results confirm that the present solution enhances diversity and inclusion in recruitment and ensures adherence to ethical and legal recruitment standards. By suppressing systemic biases and improving transparency of decisions, the GAN-based framework introduced here offers a scalable and reliable solution for AI-driven human resource management. © 2025 IEEE.},
