	affiliations = {Department of Technology and Society, Stony Brook University, Stony Brook, United States},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence is concentrating compute in hyperscale campuses and GPU clusters, pushing electricity demand, cooling intensity, and construction-related emissions far faster than legacy governance tools were designed to handle. This study develops a comparative account of how climate objectives become operational in the siting, interconnection, and procurement decisions that determine the pace and profile of new digital load. We examine four jurisdictions with contrasting power systems and administrative traditions—Northern Virginia (United States), the Nordic countries (Denmark, Finland, Norway, Sweden), Singapore, and Ireland—using a structured comparative design and within-case process tracing. The analysis triangulates statutes and regulatory directions, system-operator datasets, national statistics, and peer-reviewed research. Three institutional features consistently explain lower-carbon, grid-compatible growth under rapid scale-up: (i) vertical integration of climate targets into permits, grid access, and public procurement; (ii) horizontal coordination across energy, climate, planning, and digital authorities; and (iii) life cycle governance that shifts attention beyond facility efficiency to the embodied emissions of materials and equipment. The Nordics and Singapore show how enforceable criteria combined with clean power and procedural coherence can absorb added compute without derailing decarbonization pathways. Virginia and Ireland reveal the risks of fragmented mandates and lagging life cycle policy, including reliability stress, cost-allocation disputes, and carbon lock-in. We close with a practical blueprint that pairs hourly, locational matching of clean electricity and carbon-aware operations with ISO-aligned rules for what jurisdictions build and procure. © 20XX IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {AI infrastructure; carbon-aware computing; climate governance; comparative public administration; data centers; embodied carbon; environmental policy integration; interconnection and permitting; life cycle assessment; multi-level governance},
