	abstract = {This paper addresses the limitations of current systems in predicting possible failures during long duration missions, investigating the research question: "How can Artificial Intelligence (AI) be applied to ensure mission success?". The work produced three main outputs: a proof-of-concept AI framework to predict faults in a spacecraft's Electrical Power Subsystem (EPS); a framework for the development of an Al-enabled astronaut health assistant; and a set of international guidelines for astronaut health data privacy and security. The paper presents the outcomes of the International Space University (ISU)'s Space Studies Program 2025 (SSP25) Team Project "AI for Space Exploration", conducted by 42 participants representing 28 nationalities at Hanyang University ERICA Campus, Seoul, South Korea.This project analyzed both astronaut-centered and spacecraft-centered applications of Artificial Intelligence to assess their potential to improve long-term mission resilience and performance while addressing ethical, legal and economical impacts. The report was written during the 8-week program, by 42 people of 28 nationalities in Seoul, South Korea. In this project, the research was structured in two focus areas, or Case Studies. Firstly, Case Study 1 focused on predictive EPS maintenance, where four machine learning approaches were tested, achieving performance scores above 80% in almost all cases. The XGBoost model outperformed the others, achieving near-perfect precision and recall (0.99) in identifying early fault conditions for batteries. Secondly, Case Study 2 proposed a framework for an Al-enabled health assistant named Autonomous Resilience and Extravehicular Activity Support (ARES) with a focus on cardiovascular monitoring for the optimisation of EVA performance. A proof-of-concept MATLAB demonstration processed electrocardiogram data, achieving a 96% accuracy in the detection of arrhythmias, demonstrating the feasibility of AI-powered medical support during EVAs. In addition, the team proposed international guidelines for astronaut health data protection, management, ownership, and security, linking space law with global data protection standards, and incorporating principles for the ethical use of AI in space healthcare. The economic analysis indicated that Al-enabled predictive maintenance could lower mission costs by extending hardware lifetimes and minimizing unscheduled repairs, while autonomous health monitoring reduces the risk of medical emergencies and enhances crew safety. These findings support the integration of AI as a critical enabler of safe, resilient, and cost-effective human spaceflight. Copyright ©2025 by International Space University. All rights reserved.},
