	affiliations = {Information Technology Study Program, School of Computing, Telkom University, Jakarta Campus, Jakarta, Indonesia},
	abstract = {Healthcare cost transparency is critical for patient empowerment, yet 92% of patients lack access to accurate cost estimates before treatment, contributing to medical debt for 41% of U.S. adults. Traditional statistical methods achieve limited accuracy (R<sup>2</sup> = 0.75) in modeling complex relationships between patient factors and healthcare costs. Machine learning models like XGBoost offer superior performance but lack interpretability, a critical issue when algorithmic decisions impact patient lives. This research proposes an interpretable healthcare cost prediction system using XGBoost with dual Explainable AI (XAI) framework: SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations) for global model understanding and LIME (Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations) for patient-facing explanations. The Kaggle Insurance Cost dataset (1,338 records) was processed with domain-informed feature engineering based on WHO medical standards. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed approach outperforms other well-known ML techniques and XGBoost without optimization. The final ensemble model achieved R<sup>2</sup> = 0.8770, RMSE = $4,320, and MAE = $2,440, exceeding the project’s performance target (R<sup>2</sup> ≥ 0.87) with excellent generalization (overfitting gap = 0.0102). SHAP analysis identified smoker_bmi_interaction as the dominant cost driver ($6,397.52 mean impact), while LIME local surrogate models achieved average R<sup>2</sup> = 0.829 when approximating individual XGBoost predictions, with 8-second computation time per patient. The deployed interactive dashboard at healthcare-cost-predictor.streamlit.app quantifies actionable savings potential ($8,000-$45,200 annually) from lifestyle interventions. This framework enables transparent, evidence-based healthcare cost awareness supporting informed patient decisions. © 2025 IEEE.},
