	title = {Deconstructing AI Detection Tools: Accuracy Limits, Bias, and Human Consequences},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {2nd International Conference on Intelligent Systems for Cybersecurity, ISCS 2025},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/ISCS69371.2025.11386453},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034902002?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Apex Institute of Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Chandigarh University, Punjab, Mohali, 140413, India},
	abstract = {This is the first study to systematically evaluate AIcontent detectors using dual-direction adversarial edits across multiple genres while simultaneously addressing the ethical gray zone of human-AI collaboration. Nevertheless, there is a critical research gap, as the current literature fails to cover the Gray Zone of the human-AI collaborative authorship and does not have dual-sided adversarial appraisal methods. This study addresses the question raised in the current literature Should the text be considered AI-generated if the AI is used for minor edits. Our two-way evaluation simultaneously evaluates AI-to-human and human-to-AI adversarial changes, applying targeted and strategic changes in order to make human text look like it was produced by AI and the reverse as well with existing commercial AIDetectors as a representative system. The outcomes demonstrate how challenging it is to spot instances of collaborative authorship because even minor typographical adjustments or paraphrases reveal serious flaws in the detection techniques currently in use. Additional algorithmic constraints can be seen in the systemic variation in performance between content types as further shown by genre-specific analysis. The study shows that authorship problems continue even after grammar and style corrections, suggesting that the binary classification method is not effective to capture the complexity of modern human-AI collaboration. Apart from technical criticisms, we also draw focus on practical consequences like academic sanctions for valid work and concerns brought up by institutional policies. It is confirmed by statistical validation that these are systematic limitations rather than problems with implementation. The findings indicate that binary classification is inadequate and that detection tools ought to be reconceptualized as interpretive instruments requiring spectrumbased classifications and human oversight. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {academic integrity; adversarial robustness; AI-content detection; algorithmic fairness; detection bias; human-AI collaboration},
	keywords = {Artificial intelligence; Academic integrity; Adversarial robustness; AI-content detection; Algorithmic fairness; Algorithmics; Content detection; Detection bias; Detection tools; Gray zone; Human-AI collaboration; Ethical aspects},
