	affiliations = {Sofia University St. Kliment Ohridski, Faculty of Mathematics and Informatics, Sofia, Bulgaria},
	abstract = {The rapid integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into educational contexts has sparked both enthusiasm and concern. While these systems offer unprecedented opportunities for personalized learning, scalable tutoring, and content generation, they also introduce significant risks - including factual inaccuracies, embedded biases, pedagogical misalignment, and threats to privacy and professional roles. This paper critically examines the pitfalls of LLM-based educational tools and proposes a structured response through a tripartite framework - input, model, and output. By categorizing technical and design interventions according to the stage at which they operate, this framework provides a clear and actionable roadmap for mitigating harm and enhancing educational value. Input-level strategies focus on ethical data sourcing, inclusive prompt design, and curriculum alignment. Model-level solutions address architectural transparency, bias correction, and adaptive learning pathways. Output-level approaches emphasize human oversight, critical thinking scaffolds, and culturally sensitive content delivery. This layered analysis not only clarifies the interdependencies between technical and pedagogical challenges but also underscores the need for holistic governance. The proposed framework offers educators, developers, and policymakers a practical lens for evaluating and improving the deployment of LLMs in learning environments - ensuring that these tools support, rather than compromise, the integrity of education.  © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {AI Governance; Educational Technology; Educational Tools Design; Large Language Models (LLMs)},
	keywords = {Alignment; Artificial intelligence; Computer aided instruction; Curricula; Education computing; Engineering education; Learning systems; Teaching; AI governance; Educational context; Educational tool design; Educational tools; Language model; Large language model; Model-based OPC; Personalized learning; Professional role; Tool designs; Educational technology},
