	affiliations = {University of Information Technology, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam; Vietnam National University, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam; University of Economics and Law, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam},
	abstract = {Traditional credit scoring models systematically exclude students due to insufficient credit history, while modern AI approaches often lack transparency and introduce algorithmic bias. We propose Debate-to-Decide, a multi-agent framework that transforms credit assessment from a black-box prediction into a transparent, structured deliberation. Inspired by formal argumentation theory, the framework employs specialized Large Language Model (LLM) agents to debate conflicting points of view on an applicant's profile, resulting in a robust and well-reasoned decision. For a rigorous and privacypreserving evaluation, a synthetic student loan dataset was generated using a Conditional Tabular GAN (CTGAN). Benchmarking against a strong XGBoost baseline in predictive accuracy, algorithmic fairness, and explainability demonstrated the superior performance of Debate-toDecide in terms of accuracy and F1-score, with significantly lower geographic bias. The intrinsic explainability of the framework, provided through auditable debate transcripts, fundamentally exceeds the post hoc methods applied to black-box models. This work introduces a 'glass-box' paradigm for financial AI, enabling more accurate, fair, and transparent credit decisions, providing a practical blueprint for reliable financial technologies. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Algorithmic Fairness; Argumentation Theory; Credit Scoring; Decentralized Finance; Explainable AI; Large Language Models; Multi-Agent Systems; Synthetic Data},
