	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033154266?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Uia, Department of Ict, Grimstad, Norway},
	abstract = {In recent years, the digitalisation of power grid systems and the integration of Distributed Energy Resources (DERs) into smart grids (SGs) have increased the complexity of the electrical power ecosystem in Europe, thereby making it more vulnerable to cyberattacks. Despite the development of AI-based anomaly detection systems aimed at improving monitoring and attack identification, a significant limitation persists: such systems often fail to distinguish between cybersecurity attacks and operational safety faults or anomalies. Consequently, there is a critical need to develop more effective methods for detecting and categorising different operational conditions and failures within the power grid. However, the development of such complex systems is not feasible without adequate data and testbeds capable of simulating real-world baseline operational conditions before deployment in production environments.This paper presents a conceptual Cyber-Physical System (CPS) testbed tailored for Europe's DER-rich smart grids. The testbed integrates human operators, Artificial Intelligence (AI), safety protocols, and robust security measures. It connects a simulated SG electrical energy distribution network based on the CIGRE standard, a Human-Machine Interface (HMI) Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) control room (CR), and a Security Operations Center (SOC) at the Institute for Energy Technology. The objective is to simulate real-world conditions for electrical energy distribution and control systems under complex critical scenarios caused by operational failures and cyber vulnerabilities.First, we evaluated existing machine learning(ML)/AI-based anomaly detection methods and associated datasets in terms of accuracy and explainability in CPS environments. This evaluation revealed the absence of datasets that capture both safety-related and security-related anomalous operational conditions. Second, we conducted a systematic literature review of existing testbeds to extract key requirements necessary to simulate disruptions resulting from operational failures and cyber vulnerabilities. Identified gaps included inadequate simulation of real-world conditions, insufficient modeling of human-AI interaction, and the absence of structured root cause analysis and anomaly categorization methods. We validated the proposed conceptual framework through expert consultation with specialists in cybersecurity and power systems engineering.The proposed conceptual testbed design was made to meet the minimum requirements identified in this research work, to help create a realistic simulation of cyber vulnerabilities and operational failures, facilitating the development of anomaly detection systems that distinguish between cyber threats and operational faults. We detail the architecture, defined scenarios, and implementation, and propose a blueprint and evaluation framework using performance metrics such as realism, scalability, flexibility, and security robustness. The resulting conceptual framework demonstrates the testbed's potential to meet baseline requirements for cybersecurity and operational safety assessment in SGs. This work contributes to advancing the security and reliability of critical energy infrastructure, with global implications. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Artificial Intelligence; Cyber-Physical Systems; Distributed Energy Resources; Europe; HMI; Human-in-the-Loop; Machine Learning; Operator Training; Safety; SCADA; Security; Simulation; Smart Grids; Testbed},
	keywords = {Accident prevention; Anomaly detection; Complex networks; Control systems; Cyber Physical System; Cybersecurity; Distributed computer systems; Distributed energy; Electric power system security; Electric power transmission networks; Embedded systems; Energy resources; Human computer interaction; Learning systems; Machine learning; Man machine systems; Network security; Outages; Power control; Smart power grids; Cybe-physical systems; Cyber-physical systems; Distributed Energy Resources; Europe; Human Machine Interface; Human-in-the-loop; Machine-learning; Operator training; Security; Simulation; Smart grid; Supervisory control and data acquisition; Testbeds},
