	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031671202?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {National University, Philippines, General Education Department, Manila, Philippines},
	abstract = {This study explored how health science students at a Philippine university are adopting and using ChatGPT, applying a convergent mixed-methods approach guided by the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology 2 (UTAUT2) alongside thematic analysis of student reflections. The participants of this study were comprised of 239 undergraduate students who took part in an online survey using the UTAUT2 quantitative measures with additional open-ended questions. In results, students viewed ChatGPT as useful, convenient, and engaging, with performance expectancy, effort expectancy, hedonic motivation, and personal innovativeness receiving the highest ratings. However, behavioral intention and habit scored lowest, which suggests limited long-term commitment to its use. Using the qualitative responses, thematic analysis reinforced these findings, which show that students valued ChatGPT for improving learning efficiency, boosting confidence, and supporting independent study. Though they also raised concerns about overreliance, shallow learning, and the absence of institutional guidance. These findings highlight the importance of higher education institutions to develop clear frameworks for responsible AI integration, like embedding AI literacy in the curriculum, providing structured support, and setting ethical guidelines.  © 2025 IEEE.},
