	abstract = {At the beginning of the 20th century, submarine cables were a strategic asset for nations. After the two world wars, they became more confidential, but then they became more and more vital for billions of people, as the Internet, the economy, and everyday life became more and more intertwined. Since the aftermath of COVID-19 and February 2022, submarine cables have reemerged as strategic. Digital sovereignty is now high on the list of states, especially since the heightened gray war and the multiplication of suspicious cuts that have raised concerns about the security of the global submarine cable infrastructure. The need for a redundant and secure network has made this infrastructure a matter of concern for Western governments, especially in the European Union. New routes are needed, and the Arctic is becoming an increasingly credible route capable of securing EU data and helping to achieve digital sovereignty for Europe. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Magali Vullierme, Michael Delaunay, and Mathieu Landriault; individual chapters, the contributors.},
