	abstract = {The legal requirements for the use of AI in public administration can be divided into those relating to the deployment of AI and those relating to the development and training of AI. Deployment-related requirements for AI in public administration arise from basic constitutional rules and principles as well as from statutory laws. However, these requirements only have prohibitive effects in very specific areas of public administration. In principle, the law is largely pragmatic towards the deployment of AI in public administration. The most important development-related requirements are contained in the European AI Act. The AI Act lays down harmonised rules for AI systems. It contains protection requirements for high-risk AI systems, GPAI models, and other AI systems, promoting safe and transparent AI development. It also prohibits certain AI practices and emphasises the creation of trustworthy AI to ensure that public administrations can comply with legal and ethical standards. © The Editors and Contributors Severally 2025. All rights reserved.},
