	abstract = {Safe-by-Design and Validation and Verification (V&V) of AI/ML-based systems in aeronautics is challenging given the required levels of safety, autonomy, and the complexity of the environmental conditions. To pave the way towards their certification, certification authorities such as the European Union Aviation Safety Agency (EASA) and the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) have published their AI roadmaps, and EASA is currently running a rule making task. On the other side, standardization bodies like EUROCAE and SAE are working together on the ED-324/ARP6983, the guidelines for trustworthy AI/ML-based systems development and certification in aeronautics. This paper introduces a modular, iterative Digital Engineering Methodology based upon SysML v2 to support Safe-by-Design and V&V of AI/ML-based systems. The methodology is thoroughly applied to cover the initial design phases of a Collision Avoidance system, featuring a Neural Network model, trained with Reinforcement Learning (RL), for a remotely controlled unmanned aircraft. The Use Case demonstrates system and RL-model design and their integration, thus preparing validation and verification according to processes in upcoming standards. The approach relies upon MBSE techniques for engineering requirements, system, architecture, and ODD. This work outlines perspectives towards certification of ML-based systems in aeronautics.  © 2025 IEEE.},
