	affiliations = {Apexon, United States},
	abstract = {As mobile banking usage increases, secure and uninterrupted user authentication is becoming a fundamental challenge. Traditional login methods (e.g. password, PIN, etc.) and even static biometrics (e.g. fingerprint or face recognition) do not prevent complex threats (e.g. merry go round, take over, etc.) account takeover - identity theft. We examine the continuous authentication for mobile banking applications using AI-based behavioral biometrics (e.g. keystroke dynamics, mouse patterns, touch screen interaction). Behavioral biometrics provide continuous user verification throughout a session rather than simply one static verification. Behavioral biometrics monitor the unique, natural behaviors of users that are difficult to repeat and can offer real-time monitoring with a low level of disruption to user experience. Machine learning and anomaly detection models can detect suspicious activity in real-time to trigger automated security responses with derivatives. We offer an architecture that includes data collection, resulting feature extraction, AI model, and decision modules based on principles of active AI governance. Additionally, we discuss critical implementation complexities, such as creating ethical protocols for data collection and use, building fair and unbiased AI models to support discrimination-free modeling, ensuring transparency of the model's decision-making process, and providing clear guidance on accountability for failed systems or misuse. Our study concludes that behavioral biometrics, in conjunction with artificial intelligence and with the support of an AI governance framework, provide a deployable and low-impact opportunity to improve mobile banking security and user trust, while improving the responsible development and implementation of AI technology in high-stakes finance and regulatory compliance. © 2025 IEEE.},
