	abstract = {This paper outlines a integrated multi-modal artificial intelligence (AI) framework for the real-time detection of depression and anxiety based on the simultaneous analysis of voice, text and facial expressions. The motivation for this is the limitations of the traditional methods of diagnosis, which are reliant on subjective measurements and delayed early detection. In the proposed framework, convolutional neural networks (CNNs) are used for facial emotion recognition, the long short-term memory (LSTM) models for speech emotion analysis, and the transformer-based models including BERT for text sentiment analysis. Feature fusion is realized by a cross-modal attention mechanism to enable the model to be aware of the modality co-reliability when fusing information. Experiments were run on benchmark datasets including DAIC-WOZ and CMU-MOSEI and achieved average accuracy of 88.5% and ROC-AUC of 0.87 which is better than unimodal baselines. The model achieved a real-time inference latency of under 350 ms and can therefore be deployed in telemedicine and workplace well being systems. The proposed solution provides a resilient, explainable and scalable solution to improve mental health evaluation through multi-modal emotion analysis using milliseconds, AI Integrated models. © 2025 IEEE.},
