	abstract = {This chapter examines the generative AI (Gen AI) tools currently employed by investigative journalists covering Southern Africa and their impact through the lens of the Human-Computer Interaction (HCI) framework. The secondary interest of this study is an exploration of how the design, ownership, and deployment of Gen AI tools used by African journalists both reflect and challenge colonial legacies in technology and how these tools are being adapted to better serve the unique needs, address the distinct needs, languages, and perspectives of African journalism. The study reveals that AI tools must be adapted to African contexts, integrating local languages and cultural narratives. This means that AI development should prioritise African data sources, countering Western biases. The findings also show that this requires that journalists be trained in AI ethics, ensuring responsible Gen AI adoption. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, Trust Matsilele and Lungile A. Tshuma; individual chapters, the contributors.},
