	affiliations = {Acharya Institute of Technology, Department of Information Science and Engineering, Bengaluru, India},
	abstract = {Adaptive traffic signal control has become a critical research area to address rising congestion in urban mobility. Existing systems predominantly rely on either fixed-time signal scheduling or heuristic-based methods, which fail to adapt to dynamic traffic variations. In this work, we propose an end-to-end AI framework for adaptive traffic control that combines deep reinforcement learning (DRL) with computer vision-based traffic density estimation to dynamically optimize signal phases. Our framework introduces two core contributions: (i) a density-aware reward function that explicitly balances queue length minimization with waiting-time fairness across lanes, and (ii) a multi-source evaluation strategy that integrates simulations derived from video datasets with video-based traffic density datasets for benchmarking under diverse conditions. We establish performance baselines against fixed-time scheduling, Webster's method, and longest-queue-first policy to ensure fair comparison. Experimental results demonstrate that our approach reduces average waiting time by 27.3% over fixedtime, 19.6% over Webster's, and 14.1% over longest-queue-first, while improving throughput stability under heterogeneous traffic. Furthermore, validation using curated traffic videos shows strong agreement between model-predicted and observed congestion levels, with a 93.2% correlation (Pearson) between estimated and actual queue densities. By unifying simulation-driven learning with video-based validation, the framework provides a robust and scalable solution for adaptive traffic management, advancing research in intelligent transportation systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
