	affiliations = {Clarksburg, MD, United States; Fremont, CA, United States; Graphic Era Hill University, Department of Computer Science & Engineering, India},
	abstract = {Agentic AI systems that can pursue complex goals with minimal direct supervision-should show to be generally advantageous if we can successfully integrate them into our culture. Numerous sources and manifestations of algorithmic harm have been identified by research in the areas of fairness, accountability, transparency, and ethics. These include policing, recommendations, health care, and finance. It will take considerable work to mitigate the enormously harmful impact of such systems, especially those having an especially disproportional adverse impact on disadvantaged communities. AI systems relying on machine learning are emerging with incredibly swift speeds that change our day-to-day experience, but disadvantages as well exist. In both private and public sector contexts, scholars from diverse disciplines have increasingly raised concerns regarding algorithmic systems' privacy, transparency, bias, discrimination, exploitation, and exclusion. Focusing on the need to ensure fairness in automated consumer profiling, this research examines bias discovery and mitigation in agentic AI-driven target marketing. Initial investigation found strong biases in AI models, driven by factors such as geographic location and prior purchases, which closely map onto stored features like race and socioeconomic status. The findings of the current study substantiate existing work and demonstrate that bias in agentic AI is a persistent and multifaceted problem that requires a multi-pronged solution. The article highlights the importance of continuous audits, transparency, and crossdisciplinary collaboration in countering bias. The implications are vast for marketers, AI practitioners, and policymakers, as they point to the necessity of ethical AI use in supporting legal obligations and consumer confidence. The approach reduces bias in AI-based marketing frameworks, contributes to the wider debate on AI ethics, and promotes fairness. © 2025 IEEE.},
	author_keywords = {Agentic AI; Algorithmic system; Biasness; Mitigation; System theory},
